Short answer up front
- You’re almost certainly better off taking the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly) unless you have other large, itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, large state/local tax or property tax payments, big medical expenses). With only $800 of charitable gifts and no mortgage/property tax shown, itemizing will not exceed the standard deduction.
- Your contracting income is self‑employment income and needs Schedule C and Schedule SE; the home office deduction (you qualify if it’s used exclusively/regularly and is your principal place of business) can and should be taken against that self‑employment income. Use the actual expenses method here (your rent allocation gives a larger deduction than the simplified method).
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction but capped at $2,500 (so you cannot deduct the full $4,800). Because your total income (as shown below) is well under the phaseout range, you should be allowed the maximum $2,500.
- You will need Form 1040 plus Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if you use actual home‑office expenses), Schedule B (because your interest income > $1,500), and Schedule 1 (for adjustments like student loan interest and half of SE tax). For California, file Form 540.

Detailed breakdown and steps

1) Income summary (what you reported)
- W‑2 wages: $91,000
- Self‑employment (IT contracting): $15,000
- Interest from Marcus savings: $1,780
- Income from Interactive Brokers (interest/dividends): $480
Total gross income = $108,260

2) Treatment of the home office
- You said the space is exclusive (130 sq ft of a 780 sq ft home) — that is 130/780 = 16.667% of the home.
- Rent: $1,600/month → $19,200/year. Home‑office share of rent = 19,200 * 16.667% = $3,200/year.
- Two ways to claim:
  a) Actual‑expense method (Form 8829): you allocate actual rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., by the business percentage. Here just the rent portion alone ($3,200) exceeds the simplified method amount, so the actual method will give the bigger deduction unless your other allocable expenses are minimal or messy to support.
  b) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 130 × $5 = $650.
- Because this is self‑employment income, the home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net profit (and thus reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax). If the home office is for your W‑2 job only, it’s not deductible under current law (suspension of employee miscellaneous itemized deductions).

3) Business expenses / “unreimbursed expenses”
- Clarify: if the $650 are expenses for your contracting business (tools, software, supplies, internet used for the business, mileage, etc.), they are deductible on Schedule C.
- If the $650 are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are not deductible (suspended through tax year 2025).

4) Self‑employment tax and adjustments
- Example (estimates) using actual home‑office rent and treating the $650 as business expenses:
  - Self‑employment gross: $15,000
  - Less business expenses ($650) and home‑office allocation ($3,200) = net profit ≈ $11,150
  - SE tax (approx): 15.3% × 92.35% × net profit ≈ $1,575
  - Half of SE tax (≈ $788) is an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- That half‑SE tax deduction reduces your AGI.

5) Student loan interest
- The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment up to $2,500 (not $4,800). Because your combined income is well below the phaseout threshold, you should be eligible for the full $2,500 deduction (you’ll need Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer to support this).

6) Interest/brokerage income reporting
- Because your total interest income is $2,260 (> $1,500), you must file Schedule B with Form 1040.
- Expect Form 1099‑INT from Marcus and Form 1099 (1099‑B or 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV) from Interactive Brokers; make sure you include any broker statements for cost basis if you have sales.

7) Itemize vs. standard deduction (recommendation)
- Standard deduction for married filing jointly is substantially larger than $800 in charitable gifts. You’ll likely take the standard deduction unless you have:
  - Significant mortgage interest,
  - Large state and local taxes (note SALT capped at $10,000),
  - Large unreimbursed medical expenses exceeding the AGI threshold,
  - More charitable giving or other itemizable items.
- Items that help reduce AGI (student loan interest up to $2,500 and half of SE tax) are taken regardless of whether you itemize.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments to income: student loan interest, half of SE tax)
- Schedule B (interest/dividends > $1,500)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report contracting income and ordinary business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) if you use actual expense method for home office (if you use simplified method, you don’t use Form 8829)
- Schedule A only if you decide to itemize (likely you won’t)
- Keep Forms 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if received), W‑2, 1099‑INT, 1099‑B, 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) for your records

9) Records to gather / action items
- 1099‑NEC or records showing the $15,000 contracting income
- W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics
- 1099‑INT from Marcus, 1099s from Interactive Brokers (and 1099‑B if you sold securities)
