Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, how the home office works, whether you’ll likely itemize, and which forms you’ll need. A few quick clarifying questions (below) will let me give exact dollar guidance — otherwise I’ll show how to calculate everything with the figures you gave.

Quick summary (high level)
- Most likely you’ll take the standard deduction for 2024 unless you have significant additional itemizable expenses (mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses, or much larger charitable gifts).
- Your contracting income is treated as self‑employment income (if you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC). That income is reported on Schedule C and is eligible for a home‑office deduction (if you meet the rules).
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500 in most cases) but is subject to income phaseouts and is only for interest paid in the tax year being filed.
- Charitable donations are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses are NOT deductible for most employees since 2018; but if those $650 are self‑employment business expenses, they should go on Schedule C.

What I need to confirm
1. Was the $15,000 paid to you as independent contractor income (you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC) or as W‑2 wages? (This determines self‑employment status.)
2. The $4,800 student loan interest — did you pay that in tax years 2016–2019 and are trying to deduct retroactively, or is that interest you paid in 2024? (You can only deduct interest paid in the year you’re filing, unless you made payments in 2024 on an existing loan.)
3. The $480 from Interactive Brokers — is that interest, dividends, or capital gains? You should have 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, or 1099‑B from them.
4. Do you have mortgage interest, property taxes, or any other itemizable expenses (large medical expenses, more charitable gifts, etc.)?

Detailed guidance and how to compute

1) Income to report (from the facts you gave)
- W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics: $91,000
- Contracting income: $15,000 (report on Schedule C if self‑employed)
- Bank interest (Marcus): $1,780 — report as interest income (1099‑INT)
- Brokerage $480 — report according to the type on your broker 1099 (interest/dividend/cap gain)

2) Self‑employment tax and deductions
- If the $15,000 is self‑employment income, you file Schedule C to report gross receipts and business expenses.
- Business expenses (ordinary and necessary) reduce net profit on Schedule C and reduce both income and self‑employment tax.
- You’ll pay self‑employment tax (~15.3% on net SE income) reported on Schedule SE. Half of the self‑employment tax is an above‑the‑line deduction (reported on Schedule 1) which reduces your AGI.

3) Home office deduction (applicable only if you’re self‑employed / a sole proprietor)
You have a dedicated 130 sq ft office in a 780 sq ft rental. Two ways to compute:

- Simplified method:
  - $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft.
  - Your deduction = 130 sq ft × $5 = $650.
  - No Form 8829 needed; you report the deduction on Schedule C.

- Regular (actual expense) method:
  - Business percentage = 130 / 780 = 16.6667% (≈16.67%).
  - Apply that percentage to eligible home expenses. Rent example: $1,600/mo × 12 = $19,200 × 16.67% ≈ $3,200 allocable to the office.
  - Also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. Some items (mortgage interest, property tax) are treated specially if you own—since you rent, rent and utilities are main items.
  - You must use Form 8829 to calculate and report actual‑expense home office deduction.
  - Note: The home‑office deduction cannot exceed net profit from the business (excess may carry forward under certain rules).

Which method is better?
- Compare $650 (simplified) vs actual allocation (e.g., rent allocation alone ~$3,200 as shown). If you have meaningful utilities/insurance and net profit is sufficient, actual method will often be larger. But complexity and recordkeeping are greater. I can run the exact comparison if you want.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800)
- The student loan interest deduction generally allows up to $2,500 of interest paid in the tax year as an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1), subject to MAGI phaseouts.
- Important: Only interest paid during the tax year (e.g., payments you made in 2024) is deductible on your 2024 return. If the $4,800 represents interest from past years (2016–2019) and wasn’t paid in 2024, it’s not deductible on the 2024 return.
- Also, the deduction phases out at higher incomes (verify your MAGI against the IRS phaseout range for 2024). If your MAGI is above the phaseout range, the deduction may be reduced or eliminated.

5) Charitable contributions ($800)
- Only deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). For most married couples with only $800 in charitable gifts and no mortgage interest/property tax, the standard deduction will be larger.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If these are employee unreimbursed expenses (you are a W‑2 employee for Desert Sun), they are not deductible on federal return for most taxpayers (suspended by TCJA).
- If they are business expenses related to your contracting work, report them on Schedule C and they reduce your self‑employment net income.

7) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- For married filing jointly: standard deduction is substantially higher than the sum of the itemizable items you listed (you only listed $800 charitable + maybe limited other items). Unless you have sizable mortgage interest, property taxes, big medical expenses, or larger charitable giving, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (reduces AGI) whether you itemize or not — it does not affect the itemize decision directly.
- If you want I can run the numbers for you if you provide mortgage interest, property tax, or other potential Schedule A items.

8) Forms you’ll likely need (Federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and the deduction for half SE tax are reported here
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — if the $15k is self‑employment income
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — if you have net SE income
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use actual expenses method for home office
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you itemize (charitable gifts, mortgage interest, state/local taxes, medical, etc.)
- 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC (for contracting income), W‑2 from employer
- 1099‑INT for bank interest (Marcus) and 1099‑DIV/1099‑B or 1099‑INT from Interactive Brokers depending on the nature of the $480
- State return: California Form 540 (CA will have its own rules but generally starts with federal AGI with some adjustments)

Actions to take now (practical checklist)
- Confirm whether the $15k was 1099/self‑employment. If yes: gather receipts for business expenses (including that $650 if related to contracting).
- Get 1099s: W‑2, 1099‑NEC, 1099‑INT (Marcus), 1099 from Interactive Brokers (it will tell you the type and any cost basis info).
- Confirm how much student loan interest you actually paid in 2024 and whether you have Form 1098‑E from Cornerstone for 2024.
- Decide simplified vs actual home office method — collect rent statements, utility bills, renter’s insurance, repairs that relate to the home to support actual method if you choose it.
- If you want to lower taxable income, consider retirement contributions (SEP IRA / Solo 401(k)) for your self‑employment income — contributions reduce AGI (there are limits and deadlines; you can often set up and contribute by tax filing if done properly).

Would you like me to:
- Run estimated tax numbers (taxable income, estimated federal tax owed and self‑employment tax) using the amounts you gave and assuming the $15k is self‑employment? OR
- Help search/prepare a checklist of documents and the exact form line placements?
If you want estimated tax computations, tell me:
- Is the $15,000 1099/self‑employment?
- Did you actually pay $4,800 in student loan interest in 2024 (or is that older)?
- Any mortgage interest or property taxes paid in 2024, or other sizable deductible items?

