Thanks — I can walk you through what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, whether itemizing will make sense, what forms you’ll use, and what questions I need to give more precise numbers.

Short answer first
- You’ll almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly) rather than itemizing, because the itemizable amounts you listed are small compared with the 2024 MFJ standard deduction (about $29,200).  
- Your $15,000 IT contracting is self‑employment income — report it on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). Many deductions (home office, the $650 expenses, half of SE tax, possible QBI deduction) flow from that Schedule C.  
- Student loan interest is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (Form 1040 / Schedule 1) up to $2,500 (subject to income phase‑outs) — not an itemized deduction.  
- Charitable $800 is an itemized deduction (if you itemize), but it’s small vs the standard deduction.  
- You’ll need Form 1040 plus Schedule C, Schedule SE, Schedule B (because your interest > $1,500), and likely Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for the QBI deduction if you want the federal 20% pass‑through deduction. Also gather 1099s/1098s for reporting.

Details and how to treat each item

1) Income to report
- W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics: $91,000 — reported on Form 1040 (W‑2).  
- IT contracting $15,000 (2 clients): treated as self‑employment income — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Clients may issue 1099‑NEC; you must report the full amount even if you don’t receive 1099s.  
- Interest: Marcus $1,780 and Interactive Brokers $480 — these are taxable interest (you should receive 1099‑INT or 1099 forms). Because total interest > $1,500, you’ll complete Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends). If you have brokerage dividends or capital gains, those may add Schedule D / Form 8949 activity.

2) Home office (you rent, 780 sq ft, office = 130 sq ft exclusive)
- Because you are self‑employed, you can take a home office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business. You have two choices:
  - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft = 130 × $5 = $650 deduction.
  - Regular method: allocate actual home expenses by business percentage. Your business percentage = 130 / 780 = 16.67%. With rent $1,600/mo (annual $19,200) the rent portion allocable to business would be about $3,200 (19,200 × 16.67%). You can also prorate utilities, renter’s insurance, internet/business portion, etc. Under the regular method your total home office deduction will almost certainly exceed the simplified $650 because of the rent.
- Recommendation: use the regular method if you can document rent and utilities — it will likely produce a larger deduction. Keep utility receipts, proof of rent paid, a floorplan or notation of square footage, and dates of use.

3) Unreimbursed expenses $650
- If those are business expenses related to your contracting (supplies, software, mileage, etc.), deduct them on Schedule C (not on Schedule A). If they were unreimbursed employee expenses (i.e., you were an employee and your employer didn’t reimburse you), those are not deductible for most taxpayers under current law (TCJA suspension through 2025) unless you fall into a specific category (e.g., certain reservists, performing artists, etc.). Because you described contracting, I’m assuming they’re business expenses on Schedule C.

4) Student loan interest $4,800
- The student loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income (reported on Form 1040, Schedule 1) for interest you actually paid, but the maximum deductible per year is $2,500 (and it phases out for higher incomes). Whether you can claim the full $2,500 depends on your modified AGI (phase‑out for MFJ applies). If your joint MAGI is under the phaseout threshold, you can claim up to $2,500; if over, the deduction is reduced and may be disallowed. You’ll need Form 1098‑E from Cornerstone (or account statements) showing interest paid.

5) Charitable $800
- Cash donation to Habitat for Humanity is an itemized deduction on Schedule A. Because your likely standard deduction for MFJ (~$29,200) is much larger than $800, this alone won’t justify itemizing unless you have other large deductible items not listed.

6) Self‑employment tax and adjustments
- You’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net earnings from self‑employment (Schedule SE). SE tax is ~15.3% applied to 92.35% of net earnings. You can deduct one‑half of the SE tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1 of Form 1040.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (IRC §199A) of up to 20% of qualified business income from your contracting business (subject to limits). For your scale ($15k gross, after expenses probably smaller), a simple estimate: QBI might be roughly 20% of your net business income. The QBI deduction reduces taxable income on the federal return (but California generally does not conform to the federal QBI deduction for state income tax — check CA rules or ask your preparer).

7) Standard deduction vs itemize
- Likely standard deduction: MFJ standard deduction for 2024 is about $29,200. Your likely itemized deductions shown here are small (only $800 charitable plus maybe state/local taxes if you have any paid, which you didn’t list). Student loan interest is an adjustment and does not affect the itemize/standard decision. So in almost all cases based on what you listed, take the standard deduction.
- If you (or your spouse) have other deductible items not mentioned (large medical expenses, mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to the SALT cap, big charitable gifts, or large casualty/theft losses), add those up and compare to the standard deduction.

8) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income — student loan interest part of this; also half of SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $15,000 and related business expenses (including home office allocation if using regular method)
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — because interest > $1,500
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) for QBI deduction, if you claim QBI
- Form 1099‑NEC (received from clients) / 1099‑MISC if applicable — you don’t file these for yourself but you’ll use them to prepare forms
- 1099‑INT (Marcus, Interactive Brokers), 1099‑B (brokerage sales), 1098‑E (student loan interest) — important to have the documents
- California state return: Form 540 (or 540NR) — note: CA may not conform to some federal items (for example, QBI), so state taxable income may differ.

9) Other practical points & suggestions
- Recordkeeping: keep receipts for business expenses, rent statements, utility bills, donation receipts (you said you have the Habitat receipt), 1098‑E, 1099s, and any brokerage year‑end statements. Keep a log for any business mileage.
- Estimated tax: if you expect continued self‑employment income, you should pay estimated quarterly taxes (federal and state) to avoid underpayment penalties. Because you have $15k of self‑employment income this year, you may owe additional tax beyond what’s withheld on your W‑2; estimate and pay quarterly if necessary.
- Consider using the regular home‑office method (documentation required) because, with your rent, it will probably produce a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- CA state differences: California tax rules differ from federal in places (for example, treatment of QBI). If your state tax liability is material or you want to optimize state vs federal, ask a CA‑licensed tax preparer or accountant.

What I need from you to give exact numbers
- Does your spouse have income? (Joint return total income affects phaseouts and whether student loan interest is fully deductible.)
- Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses business expenses for your contracting work? What are they (supplies, travel, etc.)?
- Do you have utility bills or other housing costs to prorate for the home office (internet, electricity, renter’s insurance)?
- Do you receive 1099‑NEC from clients, 1099‑INT / 1099‑B, and 1098‑E from Cornerstone? If you want, upload or paste totals from those forms and I can run rough calculations: net self‑employment income, estimated SE tax, estimated federal income tax benefit vs standard deduction, and whether QBI helps.
- Do you own the home or rent? (You stated rent $1,600/month — that’s rent.)

If you want, I can:
- Run a rough numeric estimate for federal tax (showing net business income, SE tax, taxable income after standard deduction, and approximate tax owed) if you provide spouse income (if any) and confirm the items above.  
- Draft a summary email to send to a CPA with your numbers and documents attached.  

Which would you prefer next — a worked example using reasonable assumptions, or do you want me to prepare an email to send to your tax preparer listing what they’ll need?

