Thanks — I can walk you through how to treat each item, what’s deductible, whether itemizing makes sense, and which federal and California forms you’ll likely need. A few quick clarifying questions up front so I don’t make incorrect assumptions:
- Is the $4,800 in student-loan interest paid during 2024 (not prior years)? Did you receive a Form 1098‑E from Cornerstone for 2024?
- Is the $15,000 IT contracting income reported on 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, or was any of it paid through payroll? Do you have any business expenses for that contracting work (equipment, phone, software, mileage, etc.) beyond the home office?
- Do you want to use the simplified home-office method ($5/sq ft) or the actual‑expense method (share of rent, utilities, insurance, etc.)?

Summary answer first, then details.

Short summary / bottom line
- You will report: W‑2 wages ($91,000), self‑employment income ($15,000) on Schedule C, and interest income ($1,780 + $480 = $2,260) on Schedule B (because interest > $1,500).
- Home office for the contracting work is deductible on Schedule C. Two options:
  - Simplified method: 130 sq ft × $5 = $650.
  - Actual method: your business-use percentage = 130 / 780 = 16.6667%; applied to rent and eligible home expenses (so on rent alone: 16.667% × $19,200 = $3,200).
- Student loan interest: only up to $2,500 (maximum) is an above‑the‑line deduction on Schedule 1 — but it phases out at higher modified AGI. You cannot deduct $4,800 in full in one year; only interest actually paid in 2024 is eligible, and the maximum deductible amount in 2024 is $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseout).
- Charitable contribution $800 is an itemized deduction (Schedule A), but given the 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly (large — likely around $29,200), you will almost certainly take the standard deduction rather than itemize.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for the W‑2 job (the $650) are NOT deductible on the federal return for tax years 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension). Most states follow the same rule; California generally does not allow the miscellaneous unreimbursed employee expense deduction either.
- You’ll need Form(s): Form 1040, Schedule 1, Schedule B, Schedule C, Schedule SE, and either Form 8829 (actual home office) or use the simplified method entry on Schedule C. California: Form 540 (plus state schedules as required).

Detailed explanation and actions

1) Income reporting
- W‑2: Report normally on Form 1040.
- Self‑employment ($15,000): Report on Schedule C (profit or loss from business). You may have business deductions (home office, phone/computer, supplies, software, mileage) that reduce this taxable income.
- Interest/dividends: $1,780 (bank) + $480 (broker) = $2,260. Because interest > $1,500, you must complete Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) and list the payers and amounts. Include the 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B you receive.

2) Home office
- Qualification: You stated the space is exclusive and 130 sq ft — that satisfies the “exclusive and regular” use requirement for business use of the home for the contracting work.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: 130 sq ft × $5/sq ft = $650. No Form 8829 required; claim directly on Schedule C (line for home office deduction).
  - Actual expense method: Business percentage = 130 / 780 = 16.6667%. Apply that to allowable home expenses (rent, utilities, renters insurance, repairs apportioned to office, etc.). Based on rent only (rent $1,600 × 12 = $19,200) that yields 16.667% × $19,200 = $3,200 of home office rent expense (plus any share of utilities, etc. if applicable). If you use actual expenses you generally file Form 8829 to compute the deduction and carry any disallowed amounts forward.
- Note: The home-office deduction applies only to the self‑employment/contracting income (Schedule C), not to your W‑2 employment. The deduction reduces net self‑employment income, which reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax. However, the actual method/home-office expenses cannot create or increase a loss beyond allowed limits in certain situations — if your Schedule C would be a loss, there are special rules and possible carryovers. If your net self-employment profit is small, the home-office deduction using actual expenses may be limited.

3) Student loan interest
- Federal: Deductible up to $2,500 as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1). The deduction is limited and phases out at higher MAGI for married filing jointly — check current MAGI phaseout thresholds for 2024. You cannot deduct interest paid in prior years on this year’s return. You should have received Form 1098‑E from Cornerstone if you paid at least the reporting threshold; that shows interest paid.
- California: California does not conform to the federal student loan interest deduction in the same way — you should verify the CA treatment. (I can look up specifics or connect you with a tax preparer for state specifics if you want.)

4) Charitable contribution
- $800 cash donation to Habitat for Humanity is an itemized deduction on Schedule A. Because the standard deduction for MFJ is substantially larger than $800, you will almost certainly take the standard deduction for 2024 (so the $800 will not be separately beneficial unless you have many other itemizable deductions).

5) Unreimbursed employee expenses ($650)
- Federal: Not deductible for tax years 2018–2025 (suspended by TCJA). So that $650 is not deductible.
- California: Generally also disallows miscellaneous unreimbursed employee business expenses — check your situation or I can confirm.

6) Self-employment tax, and other related items
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE), roughly 15.3% on net profit (with the deductible half of SE tax reported on Schedule 1).
- You may be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income), subject to income and trade/service limitations. With relatively low self-employment income, you may qualify — it depends on taxable income and whether the business is a specified service trade or business. This is computed on Form 8995 or Form 8995‑A.
- Consider making estimated tax payments if you expect to owe tax after withholding — especially because of SE tax. If you had no quarterly payments during the year, you may owe penalties.

7) Forms you’ll typically use (Federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — adjustments to income (student loan interest up to $2,500; deduction for half of self‑employment tax is reported here as an adjustment).
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — required because interest > $1,500.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $15,000 income and business expenses including the home office deduction (or simplified home office entry).
- Schedule SE — self‑employment tax calculation.
- Form 8829 — if you elect the actual-expense method for home office. If you use simplified method, you do not file 8829.
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — for the QBI deduction if applicable.
- Keep Form(s) 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, 1099‑INT, 1099‑DIV, 1098‑E, and your W‑2 for records (you don’t “attach” most of these when e‑filing, but they are required to prepare the return and you should keep them).

8) California (state) filing
- Form 540 for California residents.
- California has differences in allowed adjustments and itemized deductions; you’ll likely take the California standard deduction (which is much smaller than federal, so state tax will still be calculated on taxable income after federal adjustments but with state rules). I can look up the exact CA standard deduction number for 2024 or refer you to a CA-specific tax resource if you want a precise comparison.

Practical recommendations and next steps
1. Likely outcome: Take the 2024 federal standard deduction for MFJ (almost certainly larger than your itemized deductions). Claim student loan interest up to $2,500 (if eligible by MAGI) as an adjustment to income, and claim the home office deduction on Schedule C (choose simplified or actual based on which gives the larger deduction; with rent-only data the actual method applied to rent gives $3,200 vs simplified $650 — but if you have no other home expenses actual might be more beneficial).
2. Gather documents: W‑2 from Desert Sun; 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for contracting clients (if applicable); 1099‑INT (bank), 1099‑DIV/1099‑B (broker), Form 1098‑E (student loan); receipts for charitable contributions and any business expenses you want to deduct.
3. Decide home-office method: If you want, give me the amounts you paid for utilities, renters insurance, repairs and I can compute the actual method total precisely and compare to the simplified method.
4. If you want, I can draft a checklist or an email to your accountant with these numbers and ask for help preparing the return. I can also search your email for key forms (e.g., “Form 1098‑E Cornerstone,” “Desert Sun Logistics W‑2,” “1099 Interactive Brokers”) if you’d like me to look through your inbox for tax docs.

