Important legal note: I’m not a CPA. Use this as practical guidance; for firm tax/filing decisions or if your situation is complex, consult a tax professional.

1) How your 2024 income gets reported
- W‑2 wages ($91,000) — reported on Form 1040 (with the W‑2 attached/entered).
- Contracting income ($15,000 from 2 clients) — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). If you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include them; even without 1099s you still report the income.
- Interest income ($1,780 Marcus savings + $480 Interactive Brokers = $2,260) — report as interest. Because your total interest > $1,500 you’ll also file Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).
- Other brokerage items — if you had dividends, capital gains, or sales reported on a 1099‑B, those go on Schedule D/Form 8949 as applicable.

2) Student loan interest ($4,800)
- The student‑loan interest deduction (up to $2,500) is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment on Schedule 1 that reduces AGI, but it applies only to interest paid in the tax year (i.e., interest you paid in 2024). You must have Form 1098‑E (or payer statement) showing interest paid in 2024.
- There’s an income phaseout based on modified AGI for MFJ (limits apply). I’ll need to know whether the $4,800 is interest paid in 2024 or earlier years; if it’s not paid in 2024 it cannot be deducted on the 2024 return.

3) Charitable contribution ($800)
- Deductible only if you itemize. Not deductible if you take the standard deduction.
- Keep your receipt — good to have the donor receipt or bank record.

4) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job: not deductible for federal tax (suspended by TCJA for 2018–2025), except for certain rare categories (e.g., qualified performing artists, certain reservists, etc.). California may differ — but for federal, generally not deductible.
- If these $650 are ordinary and necessary business expenses for your contracting work (self‑employment), they belong on Schedule C and are deductible against the $15,000 business income.

5) Home‑office (130 sq ft exclusive) — which method to use
You can only take the home‑office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business and is your principal place of business (or meets other IRS tests). Since you’re self‑employed (contracting), you qualify (W‑2 employees generally cannot claim it unless very specific conditions apply).

Two methods:

A. Simplified method
- Flat $5 per sq ft (2024), up to 300 sq ft.
- 130 sq ft × $5 = $650 home‑office deduction.

B. Actual (regular) method
- Business use percentage = home office sq ft ÷ total house sq ft = 130 / 780 = 16.667% (≈16.7%).
- Apply that percentage to home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, a portion of internet if used, etc.). You listed rent $1,600/month = $19,200/year.
- Rent portion deductible for the home office = 19,200 × 16.667% = $3,200 (plus any allocable utilities, insurance, etc., would increase the amount).
- If you use actual expenses and claim depreciation on a mortgage property, you’d need Form 4562 and Form 8829. For a renter, depreciation normally isn’t applicable, but Form 8829 is used to compute business use of home (for Schedule C).

Recommendation: Based on rent alone, the actual method (≈$3,200) is larger than the simplified ($650), so actual looks favorable. Use actual if you have utility costs and renter’s insurance to allocate as well — that will further increase the deduction. If you prefer simplicity and lower recordkeeping, use the simplified method.

Effect on self‑employment tax/AGI: The home‑office deduction (actual or simplified) reduces your Schedule C net income, which lowers income subject to self‑employment (SE) tax. Also, you will deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.

6) Self‑employment tax and other contractor items
- You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on your net Schedule C earnings. Compute this on Schedule SE. Approximately 92.35% of net earnings are subject to the 15.3% SE tax rate (but exact depends on net).
- You can deduct one‑half of your SE tax as an adjustment to income (Schedule 1).
- Business expenses (supplies, home office, phone/computer portion, mileage, etc.) reduce your net self‑employment income.
- If you expect to owe > $1,000 tax after withholding/credits, you should have made estimated tax payments during 2024 (Form 1040‑ES). If not, you may owe underpayment penalties.

7) Itemize vs standard deduction
- Standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly): $29,200.
- Itemized deductions would include: charitable contributions ($800), state and local taxes paid (SALT — state income tax withheld or state estimated payments + property taxes) up to the $10,000 SALT cap, mortgage interest (if applicable), medical expenses above 7.5% of AGI (if any), etc.
- Based only on items you listed (charitable $800), your itemized total is very likely far less than the $29,200 standard deduction — so you’d almost certainly take the standard deduction unless you have significant mortgage interest, large state and local taxes, or significant unreimbursed medical expenses.
- Action: check 2024 Form 1098 (mortgage interest) and totals for state income tax/property tax paid in 2024 to decide. If those add materially, recompute and compare to $29,200.

8) Forms you’ll likely need (Federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 — additional income and adjustments (e.g., student loan interest deduction, half of SE tax deduction appears here).
- Schedule C — to report contracting income and business expenses (home office entry is line 30; use Form 8829 if using actual expenses).
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax.
- Schedule B — if interest/dividends > $1,500 or foreign accounts.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual-expense method for business use of home.
- Form 1098‑E — for student loan interest statement (keep for your records and to support deduction).
- Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC — if you received them from clients (report income even if no 1099).
- 1099‑INT(s), 1099‑DIV, 1099‑B — from banks and brokerages.
- Form 1040‑ES — for estimated tax calculations (if you want to estimate 2025 payments).
- State return: California Form 540 (and related CA schedules). California rules differ in places; confirm CA treatment of student loan interest and employee deductions.

9) Practical checklist: documents to gather
- W‑2(s) for wages
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for contracting (or records of amounts if no 1099)
- 1099‑INT (Marcus), 1099‑DIV/1099‑B (Interactive Brokers)
- 1098‑E from Cornerstone showing interest paid in 2024
- Rent receipts/lease and utility bills (for actual home‑office method)
- Records/receipts for any business expenses (the $650 and any others)
- Charitable donation receipt for $800
- Any mortgage interest statement (1098) and property tax receipts (if applicable)
- Records of estimated tax payments (if any)

10) Follow‑up questions I need to be precise
- Was the $4,800 student loan interest paid in 2024 or earlier years? Do you have a 2024 Form 1098‑E?
- Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses for the contracting business or for your W‑2 job?
- Besides rent ($1,600/mo), do you have utilities, renter’s insurance, internet, or other home costs for 2024 you want allocated to the home office?
- Do you have mortgage interest or property tax payments in 2024 (or state income tax paid) that could materially increase itemized deductions?
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC forms from clients? Any 1099‑B or other brokerage statements?
- Do you want a rough numeric estimate of your 2024 taxable income and self‑employment tax using the assumptions we discussed? If so I can calculate an approximate tax/SE tax and show the effect of actual vs simplified home‑office.

11) Quick illustrative example (approximate) — to show effect of home office
(Assume: $15,000 contracting income; $650 expenses relate to contracting; use rent only as home expense; no other business expenses for this example)
- Business gross = $15,000
- Home office actual (rent allocation) = $3,200
- Other business expenses = $650
- Net Schedule C income = 15,000 − 3,200 − 650 = $11,150
- SE tax base ≈ 11,150 × 0.9235 = $10,302 → SE tax ≈ $10,302 × 0.153 = ~$1,576
- One‑half SE tax deduction ≈ $788 (adjustment on Schedule 1)
- Net effect: your AGI will include roughly $91,000 + $11,150 + $2,260 interest − adjustments (half SE + any student loan interest allowed) — this is just an illustration. If you used simplified home office instead, your net Schedule C would be $15,000 − 650(simplified) − 650(other) = $13,700, increasing SE tax and AGI.

