1) Key facts / assumptions I used
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (MFJ) for tax year 2024.
- Income you reported: W‑2 wages $91,000; self‑employment / contracting gross $15,000 (I’m assuming you were paid as an independent contractor / 1099 work); interest income $1,780 (Marcus) + $480 (Interactive Brokers) = $2,260.
- You listed $4,800 student loan interest — I assume that is the interest paid in 2024. (See questions below to confirm.)
- Home: rented, 780 sq ft; exclusive home office 130 sq ft.
- Rent: $1,600/month → $19,200/year.
- Charitable cash: $800 (you have a receipt).
- “Unreimbursed expenses” $650 — I need to know whether these are expenses related to the contracting work (self‑employment) or unreimbursed W‑2 employee expenses.

2) What you can deduct (and what you cannot)
- Self‑employment business expenses: If the $15,000 IT contracting income is self‑employment income (1099), legitimate business expenses related to that contracting work are deductible on Schedule C. That includes:
  - Home office deduction (see below).
  - Business supplies, software, mileage, phone/internet portion used for the business, etc.
  - The $650 “unreimbursed expenses” can be deducted on Schedule C only if they were business expenses for the contracting work. If they were unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible (misc. employee expenses were suspended by TCJA through 2025).
- Home office:
  - Allowed only if you use the space “regularly and exclusively” for business and you are self‑employed or meet very narrow exceptions for employees (generally not allowed for W‑2 workers).
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5.00 per sq ft × 130 sq ft = $650 (max 300 sq ft).
    - Actual (recommended in your case): allocate a portion of rent and allowable home expenses to the office. Rent portion = (130 / 780) × $19,200 = $3,200. You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renters insurance, repairs, and depreciation if owning. For a renter, the rent share alone ($3,200) already exceeds the simplified method ($650).
  - If you use the actual method, you’ll calculate it on Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and carry that to Schedule C.
  - Home office deductions reduce your Schedule C net profit and therefore reduce both income tax and self‑employment tax (to the extent they reduce net SE income).
- Self‑employment tax & deductions:
  - You’ll owe self‑employment (SE) tax on net self‑employment income (Schedule SE). You can deduct one‑half of SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Student loan interest:
  - Student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (Schedule 1) up to $2,500 (not $4,800). So even if you paid $4,800 interest, the maximum deduction is $2,500, subject to MAGI phase‑outs. For your income level (W‑2 $91k + net self‑employment ~somewhere up to $15k less business expenses), you are very likely below the 2024 phaseout threshold for MFJ — so you would typically get the full $2,500 (but I’ll confirm if you want me to check using exact MAGI thresholds).
