- Unreimbursed employee expenses: not deductible for federal taxes (unless you are a qualified performing-artist or certain other narrow categories — unlikely).

3) Itemize vs. standard deduction (quick recommendation)
- 2024 standard deduction (MFJ): $29,200.
- Based on what you gave (charitable $800, probable SALT maybe a few thousand if state income tax withheld, and no mortgage interest), your likely total itemized deductions are well under $29,200. So it will almost certainly be better to take the standard deduction for your personal deductions.
- Crucial point: “Taking the standard deduction” is independent of deducting business expenses. You still claim Schedule C expenses, home office, half SE tax, student loan interest (above the line), etc., regardless of whether you itemize or not. The choice to itemize only affects Schedule A deductions (charity, SALT, mortgage interest, medical if large, etc.).
- Recommendation: Likely take the standard deduction and claim your contracting expenses and home office on Schedule C (actual method looks more favorable given the rent share of $3,200 > $650 simplified). If you have large state tax or mortgage interest not listed here, re-check.

4) Estimated numbers (very rough) to show impact
- Gross income approx: $91,000 + $15,000 + $2,260 = $108,260.
