  - Self‑employment tax (approx): 92.35% × $11,800 × 15.3% ≈ $1,667 (Schedule SE).
  - Half SE tax deduction ≈ $834 (Schedule 1).
  - Student loan interest deduction (max allowed) = $2,500 (if eligible).
  - Adjusted gross income (rough) ≈ $108,260 − $834 − $2,500 = $104,926.
  - Minus standard deduction $29,200 → taxable income ≈ $75,726 (very approximate).
- If instead you used simplified home office ($650) net SE income is larger and SE tax a bit higher, and AGI slightly different. The point: actual method with rent share gives a larger business deduction in your case.

5) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — for student loan interest deduction (and other adjustments).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — reports the $15,000 contracting income and business expenses (home office, other expenses).
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the actual method for home office; if you use simplified method, you do not file 8829, just report the simplified deduction on Schedule C.
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax.
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — to claim the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (up to 20% of qualified business income) for the contracting income. Usually Form 8995 (simpler) is fine unless you hit phaseout rules or have other complexities.
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (it sounds unlikely, but keep it in mind).
- Supporting info you should have/expect:
  - W‑2 from Desert Sun Logistics.
  - 1099‑NEC (or 1099‑MISC) from your two contracting clients.
  - 1099‑INT from Marcus (and any bank) showing $1,780.
  - 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B or consolidated statement from Interactive Brokers for the $480 (or capital gains/dividends; include any will be on 1099s).
