Quick headline recommendation
- Most likely you will take the standard deduction for 2024 as married filing jointly, unless you have significant other itemizable items (mortgage interest, large property taxes, big state tax withholding, large medical expenses, etc.). With the numbers you gave, your available itemized deductions appear small.
- Your contracting income should be reported on Schedule C (self‑employment). The home‑office deduction is allowed for that self‑employment income (not for your W‑2 job) and will reduce your net self‑employment income and self‑employment tax.
- Student loan interest can often be deducted “above the line” (adjustment to income) up to $2,500 (subject to income limits), not as an itemized deduction — but I need to confirm the $4,800 figure represents interest you paid in 2024 (not earlier years).

1) What you can deduct (federal)
- W‑2 wages: Not deductible; report on Form 1040 (W‑2 attached).
- Contracting income ($15,000): Report on Schedule C. Against that you can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses related to contracting (see below).
  - Home‑office deduction: Allowed for the contracting/self‑employment work if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business.
  - Unreimbursed expenses ($650): If those expenses relate to your self‑employed contracting work (e.g., supplies, software, travel for clients), they are deductible on Schedule C. If those $650 are unreimbursed expenses from your W‑2 employer, they are NOT deductible (suspension of miscellaneous itemized employee expenses under the TCJA, through 2025).
- Student loan interest ($4,800): The student loan interest deduction is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1) and is limited to $2,500 per tax year per return, subject to modified adjusted gross income (MAGI) phaseouts. Also, the interest must have been paid in the tax year (2024) and be on a qualified student loan. If the $4,800 is interest paid in 2024, you can likely deduct up to $2,500 (phased out at higher incomes). If those interest amounts were paid in years 2016–2019, they are not deductible for 2024.
- Charitable donation ($800): Cash gift to a qualified charity is an itemized deduction. No deduction if you take the standard deduction (there is no current general above‑the‑line charitable deduction for 2024).
- Interest from Marcus and Interactive Brokers ($1,780 + $480 = $2,260): Taxable interest — must be reported on Form 1040. If total interest (and ordinary dividends) exceed $1,500, you’ll file Schedule B.
- State and local tax (SALT): You can choose to deduct state income tax or state/local sales tax and property tax, but SALT is capped at $10,000 total for federal itemized deductions. You didn’t list state taxes or property taxes — include them if you paid substantial amounts.

2) Home‑office calculation (you gave: 130 sq ft exclusive; home 780 sq ft; rent $1,600/month)
Two methods — you choose the larger (actual-expense) or simplified method:

- Actual (regular) method
  - Business percentage = 130 / 780 = 16.666…% ≈ 16.67%
  - Annual rent = $1,600 × 12 = $19,200
  - Home office portion of rent = 19,200 × 16.67% ≈ $3,200
  - You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs that benefit the office, etc., by the same percentage. Use Form 8829 to calculate if you claim the actual method.
  - Result: this reduces your Schedule C gross income (so also reduces self‑employment tax).

- Simplified method
  - $5.00 per square foot × 130 sq ft = $650 (max 300 sq ft).
  - No Form 8829 necessary; enter the simplified deduction on Schedule C.

Recommendation: based on rent alone, the actual method gives ~ $3,200 vs $650 simplified; the actual method is usually better here unless you have reasons to prefer simplified method (simplicity/no recordkeeping).

Important home‑office rules
- Must be used regularly and exclusively for business (no personal use).
- Must be your principal place of business (or used to meet clients/customers).
- Applies only to self‑employment income (Schedule C). If you are a W‑2 employee and your employer required you to work from home but didn’t reimburse you, you generally cannot take this deduction for 2018–2025.

3) Itemize vs. standard deduction (estimate)
- For 2024 married filing jointly the standard deduction is (approximately) $29,200. Please confirm the exact 2024 IRS amount in your final filing (IRS site or your tax software will have the precise figure).
- Your likely itemized deduction components (from info provided): charitable $800 + possibly state income tax/property tax (unknown) + mortgage interest (none listed) + medical (none listed). Without significant other itemized amounts, your itemized total likely will be well under the standard deduction, so the standard deduction is probably better.
- Student loan interest does NOT factor into the itemize vs standard comparison because it’s an above‑the‑line adjustment.

4) Forms you’ll most likely need (Federal)
- Form 1040 — individual tax return (married filing jointly).
- Form(s) W‑2 — Desert Sun Logistics (attach copy).
- Schedule B — Interest and Ordinary Dividends (required if total interest/dividends > $1,500). Your interest = $2,260 so Schedule B is required.
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report $15,000 contracting income and business expenses).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual method for the home office). If you use simplified method, you do not use Form 8829.
- Schedule SE — Self‑Employment Tax (calculate SE tax on net Schedule C profit).
- Schedule 1 (Adjustments to Income) — to report the student loan interest deduction (above‑the‑line), and other adjustments (half of self‑employment tax deduction also flows here).
- Form 1099‑NEC / 1099‑MISC — you may receive these from clients; even if you don’t receive them, you must report the income.
- Form 1040‑ES — for estimated tax payments, if you need to make quarterly estimated payments for your self‑employment income (if significant tax/SE is due).
- California: Form 540 (CA resident return). California may have different rules or adjustments (e.g., student loan interest conformity, home‑office treatment). Keep CA tax considerations in mind.

5) Other tax considerations & recordkeeping
- Self‑employment tax: On net self‑employment income you pay SE tax (~15.3% on 92.35% of net profit). Half of SE tax is an above‑the‑line deduction.
- Retirement contributions: You can reduce taxable income by contributing to a SEP‑IRA or Solo 401(k) if you qualify; this may be useful to lower taxable/self‑employment income.
- Estimated payments: If you expect substantial tax due because of the $15k self‑employment and little withholding, consider making estimated payments during the year to avoid penalties.
- Keep receipts and records: for the home office (floorplan/photos, calculation of square footage), charitable receipts, interest statements (1099‑INT), 1099‑NEC, and business expense receipts.
- State differences: California generally conforms to many federal rules but has differences — check CA Form 540 instructions. For example, CA may not allow certain federal adjustments exactly as federal does.

6) Clarifying questions I need to finalize guidance
- Does the $4,800 student loan interest represent interest you actually paid in 2024? Or are those totals from earlier years (2016–2019)? If paid in earlier years, they are not deductible in 2024.
- Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses related to your contracting (self‑employment) work or to your W‑2 job?
- Do you have other itemizable amounts (mortgage interest, property taxes, large state income tax paid, large medical expenses) not listed?
- Did you receive 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC from your two contracting clients? If not, do you know the gross amounts?
- Do you want an estimated tax calculation (whether you should make quarterly payments), and/or a rough federal tax estimate for 2024?

If you want, I can:
- Run quick sample numbers using current standard deduction amount (I’ll mark where I used assumed official amounts), or
- Draft a checklist email you can send to your tax preparer with the items they’ll need, or
- Help you prepare an email to your clients asking for 1099s or clarifying payments.

