Thanks — I can walk through this and tell you what’s deductible, how to treat the home office, whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, and which federal & California forms you’ll need. I’ll also flag a few clarifying questions at the end that could change the recommendations.

Summary of your numbers (as you gave them)
- W-2 wages (Desert Sun Logistics): $91,000
- Self-employment / IT contracting: $15,000 (2 clients)
- Interest from Marcus savings: $1,780
- Interest / investment proceeds from Interactive Brokers: $480
- Rent: $1,600/month → $19,200/year for a 780 sq ft unit
- Home office: 130 sq ft exclusive use for business
- Student loan interest: $4,800 (you noted “Cornerstone, 2016–2019” — see clarifying Qs)
- Charitable cash contributions: $800 (you have a receipt)
- “Unreimbursed expenses”: $650

What is deductible (federal) — quick list and treatment
- Self-employment business expenses (Schedule C)
  - The $15,000 contracting is taxable self-employment income and should be reported on Schedule C. Any ordinary and necessary business expenses related to that contracting (supplies, phone, software, travel, part of your home office, etc.) are deductible on Schedule C and reduce both income tax and self-employment tax.
  - The $650 “unreimbursed expenses”: if these were expenses you incurred for your W-2 job, they are generally NOT deductible for most taxpayers (suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions through 2025). If they relate to your contracting work, they belong on Schedule C and are deductible.
- Home office deduction (self-employed only)
  - You qualify provided the 130 sq ft space is used exclusively and regularly for the contracting business (you said exclusive). You have two options:
    - Simplified method: $5 per sq ft × 130 sq ft = $650 (no Form 8829; easy).
    - Regular method: allocate actual home expenses to the business. Allocation = business sq ft / total sq ft = 130 / 780 ≈ 16.67%. With rent $19,200/year, the business share of rent alone ≈ $3,200. If you also allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., the deductible amount will likely be larger than $650. The regular method requires Form 8829 (Business Use of Your Home) and more recordkeeping, but often gives a larger deduction for renters.
  - Important: this deduction only reduces your Schedule C net income (and so reduces self-employment tax and income tax). W-2 employees generally cannot take a home office deduction.
- Self-employment tax
  - You’ll owe self-employment (SE) tax on net earnings from self-employment (Schedule SE). SE tax is ~15.3% on net earnings; half of SE tax is deductible as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Interest and investment income
  - The Marcus interest ($1,780) and Interactive Brokers ($480) are taxable interest/dividend income. Because total interest ($2,260) is over $1,500, you’ll likely need to file Schedule B with Form 1040 (and include any dividend reporting or foreign account questions).
- Student loan interest
  - The student loan interest deduction is an above-the-line adjustment (Schedule 1), up to $2,500 per year, subject to income limits (phaseout based on MAGI). You cannot deduct more than the interest you actually paid in the tax year — and the maximum federal deduction is $2,500. You mentioned $4,800 — that exceeds the annual limit and/or may reference interest paid in earlier years; I need to confirm which year(s) the payments were made. If you actually paid $2,500+ in 2024 and your MAGI is below the phaseout, you can deduct up to $2,500 for tax year 2024.
- Charitable contribution ($800)
  - Cash contributions to qualified charities are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). You have the receipt, which is good (for cash gifts $250+ you need written acknowledgement).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (W-2)
  - Generally not deductible under current law (suspended through 2025). If these were business expenses for your contracting work, put them on Schedule C instead.

Itemize vs. standard deduction (likelihood)
- For tax year 2024 the standard deduction for married filing jointly increased (roughly in the high $20k range; confirm current IRS number when you file), so to benefit from itemizing your Schedule A deductions generally need to exceed that standard amount.
- Your itemizable amounts you listed: $800 charitable. Student loan interest is an adjustment (not an itemized deduction). Unreimbursed employee expenses generally not deductible. Unless you have significant mortgage interest, large state/local taxes (up to the SALT cap), medical expenses above the AGI threshold, or other large itemizable items you didn’t list, you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction rather than itemizing.
- Conclusion: Most likely take the standard deduction for federal purposes; however, the home office deduction is separate and reduces your Schedule C income whether or not you itemize.

Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments such as half of SE tax, student loan interest (line depends on tax year form layout)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — to report $15,000 contracting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax
- Schedule B — if your taxable interest & dividend income exceeds $1,500 (you have ~$2,260)
- Form 8829 — if you choose the regular method for the home office deduction (not required for simplified method)
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (probably not, based on amounts listed)
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC — you should collect any 1099s received from clients (they are informational; you must report income even if you didn’t receive 1099s)
- 1099-INT, 1099-DIV, 1099-B from banks and brokers — for interest and investment income (Interactive Brokers may issue 1099-B for sales and 1099-INT/1099-DIV)
- Form 1040-ES — for making estimated tax payments during the year (if you expect tax liability from self-employment with no/insufficient withholding)

California state forms (CA)
- California Form 540 (Resident) — report federal AGI and adjustments per California rules
- California schedule for business income — California generally taxes net self-employment income; you may need state-specific adjustments
- California estimated tax vouchers if you owe and didn’t have sufficient withholding

Other practical notes and tax-planning items
- Student loan interest: confirm how much interest you actually paid in 2024. Only interest paid during the tax year is deductible and capped at $2,500 (subject to MAGI phaseouts for MFJ).
- Home office — if you rent, the regular method will likely yield a larger deduction than the $650 simplified amount because of your $19,200 rent (≈$3,200 business portion) — but only if you have the records and want to file Form 8829. If you prefer simpler recordkeeping or if the home office deduction would be small once you account for other factors, the simplified method is fine.
- Make sure your contracting clients issued any 1099s they were required to. Regardless of 1099s, you must report all self-employment income.
- Self-employment tax and estimated payments: because you have $15k self-employment income with (likely) little/no withholding, plan for SE tax and income tax. You may need to make estimated federal and California payments (Form 1040-ES and CA vouchers) to avoid underpayment penalties.
- Interactive Brokers: if you have investment sales, you may have capital gains/losses reported on Form 1099-B. The $480 could be interest, dividends, or proceeds — confirm what it is and keep the brokerage statements.

Clarifying questions I need to give precise answers
1. Did you actually pay the $4,800 student loan interest during tax year 2024, or is that the total interest paid across 2016–2019? (Only interest paid in the tax year is deductible; max deductible is $2,500.)
2. Do the $650 “unreimbursed expenses” relate to your W-2 job (not deductible) or to your contracting business (deductible on Schedule C)?
3. Do you rent (it looks like it from $1,600/mo) or own the home? (You said you pay $1,600/month — I assume rent.)
4. Do you want to use the simplified home office method for ease, or are you willing to calculate actual allocated rent/utilities and file Form 8829?
5. Did you receive 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC from the clients? Do you have 1099-INT/1099-DIV/1099-B from Marcus or Interactive Brokers?

Would you like:
- A sample calculation comparing simplified vs regular home office deduction (I can compute a rough tax impact if you give utilities & other home expenses)?
- A checklist of documents to gather for filing or for handing to a preparer?
- A draft email to your CPA listing these items and asking for preparation/confirmation?

If you confirm the clarifying items above I’ll calculate estimated taxable income, approximate SE tax, and give a recommended filing approach (including whether the regular home-office method likely beats simplified for your situation).

