I can walk you through what’s deductible, whether to itemize, how to treat the home office, and which federal and California forms you’ll likely need — plus a few questions so I can be more precise.

Short answers up front
- Likely better to take the standard deduction (2024 MFJ = $29,200) unless you have large additional itemizable items (SALT, mortgage interest, big medical expenses).
- Your $15,000 IT contracting is self‑employment income and belongs on Schedule C; that income is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).
- Home office is deductible only against your self‑employment income (not your W‑2). You can use the simplified method (130 sq ft × $5 = $650) or actual-expense method (a % of rent/utilities). Use whichever gives the larger/cleaner deduction.
- Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line deduction (up to $2,500 per year, subject to income limits) — it reduces AGI whether or not you itemize.
- Charitable $800 is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A).
- Unreimbursed employee expenses (post‑TCJA) are not deductible for 2024; if the $650 are business expenses for your contracting work, include them on Schedule C.

Key items and how to treat them (details & estimates)
1) Income
- W-2 from Desert Sun Logistics: $91,000 — reported on Form 1040 (W-2).
- Contracting: $15,000 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business). If you received 1099-NEC or 1099-MISC, include those; if not, you still report the income.
- Interest: $1,780 (Marcus) — generally 1099‑INT, report on 1040.
- $480 from Interactive Brokers — could be interest, dividends, or capital gains. Report per the form(s) 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B you receive.

2) Self-employment tax and related adjustments
- Net profit from Schedule C is subject to self‑employment tax. You’ll compute Schedule SE and pay SE tax (15.3% on ~92.35% of net SE earnings).
- You can deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- You may also qualify for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) — roughly up to 20% of qualified business income from the Schedule C, subject to income limits; with your income levels you’re likely eligible for the simple 20% QBI deduction, but it depends on taxable income and specifics.

3) Home office
- Eligibility: Must be used regularly and exclusively for your contracting business. W-2 employees cannot claim this for 2018–2025; but self-employed people can.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 (no Form 8829 required).
  b) Actual-expense method: allocate a percent of rent/utility/insurance/repairs to the home office. Your allocation = 130 / 780 ≈ 16.67%. Annual rent = $1,600 × 12 = $19,200; rent portion for office ≈ $3,200. Add your portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc. If you use actual you’ll complete Form 8829 (business use of home) and carry the allowed amount to Schedule C.
- Note limitation: the home-office deduction cannot increase or create a net loss from the business for purposes of the overall self‑employment loss rules; excess home-office deduction may be carried forward in some cases.

4) Student loan interest
- Max deduction is $2,500 per year (above-the-line on Schedule 1), subject to MAGI phaseouts. You indicate $4,800 — clarify whether that $4,800 was paid in 2024 or is a total outstanding. Only the interest actually paid in 2024 (and limited to $2,500) is potentially deductible. You should have Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer if interest paid ≥ $600 (but you can claim the deduction even if no 1098‑E was issued).

5) Charitable gift ($800)
- Cash donation to a qualified charity is deductible if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt/proof. If you take the standard deduction, you don’t separately deduct that $800.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If these are unreimbursed employee expenses (e.g., for a job where you’re a W‑2 employee), they’re not deductible for 2018–2025.
- If they are ordinary and necessary business expenses incurred in your contracting work, include them on Schedule C.

Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2024 standard deduction (Married Filing Jointly) = $29,200.
- Your stated itemizable items are $800 charitable + whatever state/local taxes you paid (SALT capped at $10,000) + any mortgage interest (none mentioned). Unless you have significant additional itemizable deductions (large state income taxes paid, property tax, mortgage interest, big medical expenses), you will almost certainly be better off taking the standard deduction.

Forms you’ll likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return).
- Schedule 1 (adjustments to income) — for student loan interest deduction, half SE tax adjustment, other adjustments.
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report the $15,000 contracting income and business expenses (including home office and any business supplies).
- Schedule SE — compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual-expense method for home office (not required for simplified).
- Form 8995 (or 8995‑A) — for Qualified Business Income deduction (8995 is the simpler form if you qualify).
- Schedule A — only IF you itemize.
- 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if issued by clients), 1099‑INT (Marcus), brokerage 1099s (Interactive Brokers — 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV/1099‑B as applicable).
- Form 1098‑E for student-loan interest (if issued).

California (state) forms
- California Form 540 (resident return).
- CA has its own rules for adjustments; you’ll report the same income on CA return (CA does not have a separate self‑employment “SE tax” but the income is subject to CA income tax). Check California guidance for whether they conform to the federal QBI deduction (CA historically did not conform; confirm current year rules or ask a CA tax preparer).

Recordkeeping and receipts
- Keep W‑2, 1099s, 1098‑E, bank/brokerage statements, Habitat receipt, rent statements/leases and utility bills (for home office), receipts for business expenses, and records supporting the 130 sq ft exclusive-use claim.

Estimate examples (rough)
