1) Income summary (for context)
- W‑2 wages: $91,000
- Self‑employment (IT contracting): $15,000 (report on Schedule C)
- Interest: $1,780 (Marcus) + $480 (Interactive Brokers) = $2,260
Estimated total income (before adjustments): $108,260.

2) What you can (likely) deduct
- Self‑employment business expenses (Schedule C)
  - The $650 of unreimbursed expenses: deductible on Schedule C only if they are ordinary and necessary business expenses for your contracting work. If they are unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are not deductible under current law.
  - Any other business costs (software, supplies, mileage, phone portion, etc.) should also go on Schedule C.

- Home office (if used for the contracting/self‑employment work)
  - You say 130 sq ft exclusive use; home is 780 sq ft → business-use percent = 130/780 = 16.667%.
  - Two methods:
    - Regular method: prorate actual home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance portion, etc.). With rent $1,600/month ($19,200/year), the rent portion allocated to the office would be ~16.667% × $19,200 = $3,200. If you have utilities or other qualifying expenses, add the prorated share as well.
    - Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 flat deduction.
  - The regular method produces a larger deduction here (rent portion ~$3,200 > $650). The home office deduction applies only to the business reported on Schedule C (self‑employment). It does not apply if you only have a W‑2 job and your employer does not reimburse you.

- Self‑employment tax deduction
  - You must pay self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings. You can deduct one‑half of the self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1).

- Student loan interest
  - The maximum student loan interest deduction is $2,500 per year (an above‑the‑line adjustment to income). It phases out at higher MAGI levels (income limits apply). With your stated income around $108k, you are likely under the phase‑out for MFJ, but I’ll need to confirm the exact MAGI thresholds for the year. Important: you can only deduct interest actually paid during the tax year (and you should have Form 1098‑E from the servicer). Your note “Cornerstone, 2016–2019” is ambiguous — if the $4,800 is interest paid over prior years, you cannot deduct that on the 2024 return. Clarify this (see my question below).

- Charitable contributions
  - $800 cash to Habitat for Humanity is deductible if you itemize. For any single contribution greater than $250 you must have a proper written acknowledgement from the charity (you said you have a receipt — keep it).
  - If you take the standard deduction, the $800 gives you no additional federal deduction (except certain limited provisions that sometimes allow limited above‑the‑line donations; generally not applicable to most taxpayers for 2024).

- Interest income reporting
  - The $2,260 of interest is taxable and must be reported. Because total interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500, you will likely need Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).

3) Itemize vs. standard deduction
- For 2024 the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is around $29,200 (confirm the IRS published update at filing time).
- Items you gave that would be on Schedule A (itemized):
  - Charitable: $800
  - State & local taxes (SALT): you can deduct state income taxes or property taxes up to $10,000 combined — you did not give state tax paid or property tax paid, so cannot count it yet.
  - Mortgage interest / medical expenses / other itemized categories: you didn’t list any.
- Because your listed itemizable deduction (charity $800) is well below the standard deduction, you will most likely take the standard deduction unless you have substantial state income tax, property tax, or mortgage interest that wasn’t mentioned. Student loan interest is above‑the‑line (not an itemized deduction), so you get that whether or not you itemize (subject to phaseouts).

My strong likelihood: take the standard deduction and also claim the student loan interest adjustment (up to $2,500) and all Schedule C business deductions (including home office). That usually yields a better result than itemizing for the facts you provided — but confirm your total SALT and any other deductible items before deciding.

4) Forms you’ll need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income: e.g., one‑half of self‑employment tax deduction and the student loan interest deduction are entered via Schedule 1.
- Schedule B — if interest + ordinary dividends > $1,500 (you have $2,260, so likely required).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your $15,000 contracting income and associated business expenses (including the home office deduction if you use the simplified method; for the regular method you’ll calculate via Form 8829).
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings.
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you choose the regular method for the home office deduction. If you choose the simplified method, you do not file Form 8829; instead you report the simplified amount on Schedule C.
- Keep Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement) from your loan servicer to substantiate the student loan interest deduction (if the interest you claim was actually paid in 2024).
- Form(s) 1099‑INT and 1099‑B (from banks and brokerages) and 1099‑NEC (if clients issued you 1099s). Even if you didn’t receive a 1099‑NEC, you still must report the $15,000 of contracting income.

State:
- California Form 540 (or CA return) — file jointly with spouse. State rules for deductions and credits differ; CA conforms to many federal rules for business income but you still must prepare a CA return.

5) Examples / quick calculations (illustrative)
- Home office (regular method): rent portion ~ $3,200/year (16.667% × $19,200).
