Thanks — that’s enough to give clear, practical guidance and next steps. A few quick clarifying questions before I finalize anything:
- Did you actually pay the $4,800 in student-loan interest during 2024, or does “2016–2019” refer to the loan dates? (The deduction is for interest you paid during the tax year.)
- Are the $650 “unreimbursed expenses” expenses related to your IT contracting (your self‑employment)? Or are they unreimbursed employee expenses from your W-2 job? (That affects whether they’re deductible.)
- Do you have mortgage interest, property taxes, or other potentially large itemizable expenses you didn’t list?

Below is a clear summary of how each item should be handled, plus recommendations and the forms you’ll likely need.

1) Income reporting
- W-2 wages from Desert Sun Logistics: report on Form 1040 (W-2 income goes on Form 1040 automatically).
- IT contracting $15,000: report on Schedule C (Form 1040) as self‑employment business income. If you received 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC, include those; even if you didn’t receive 1099s, you still report the income.
- Interest income: Marcus ($1,780) and Interactive Brokers ($480) are taxable interest; report on Schedule B if total interest/dividends exceed the threshold or directly on Form 1040 if not.

2) Self‑employment taxes and adjustments
- Self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) applies to net income from the contracting work. You calculate this on Schedule SE and pay the tax; you can deduct one-half of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (reduces AGI).
- Business expenses that are ordinary and necessary for the contracting work are deductible on Schedule C (e.g., software, supplies, business travel). If the $650 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses for the contracting, move them to Schedule C. If they were unreimbursed employee expenses from your W‑2 job, they are not deductible for 2018–2025 under current law.

3) Home office
- You can only claim a home office deduction for the portion of your home used regularly and exclusively for the self‑employment/business activity.
- Your numbers: home office = 130 sq ft; total home = 780 sq ft → business use percentage = 130/780 = 16.67%.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5.00 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction directly on Schedule C. No Form 8829 required.
  b) Regular (actual) method: allocate a percentage (16.67%) of home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) to the business. With rent $1,600/mo = $19,200/yr, the rent portion allocable to the home office would be about $3,200 (0.1667 × $19,200), plus a portion of utilities, etc. To use the actual method you generally use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) with Schedule C and you may need Form 4562 if claiming depreciation.
- Recommendation: For your numbers, the simplified method yields $650. The actual method (rent share alone) would give about $3,200 from rent, so actual method could be larger — but actual method requires careful allocation and paperwork, and the deduction for home office cannot exceed the gross income from that business. If your contracting net is small or you have few other business expenses, run the math both ways. I can do that calculation if you confirm the $650 unreimbursed expenses are business related and any other home expenses (utilities, renter’s insurance) you want included.

4) Student loan interest ($4,800)
- The student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (up to $2,500 max) for interest you actually paid in the tax year. It phases out at higher MAGI levels. If your $4,800 is interest paid in 2024, only up to $2,500 may be deductible and only if your modified AGI is below the phaseout threshold. If the $4,800 was interest paid in earlier years (2016–2019) you cannot deduct it on your 2024 return — only interest paid in the tax year being filed is deductible.
- You should receive Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer showing interest paid in 2024 (if any) — keep that for your records.

5) Charitable donation $800
- Charitable contributions are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Keep the receipt. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot separately deduct this on your federal return (unless special rules apply for a tax year).

6) Itemize vs Standard deduction
- For 2024, the standard deduction for married filing jointly is $29,200. (If you want, I can confirm the current IRS figure at filing time, but that is the 2024 amount.)
- To benefit from itemizing, your total itemized deductions (state and local taxes up to the $10,000 SALT cap, mortgage interest, property taxes, charitable contributions, large medical expenses over 7.5% of AGI, etc.) must exceed $29,200.
- Based on what you listed (only $800 charitable + possibly some state tax withheld), you will probably be better off taking the standard deduction unless you also have mortgage interest, large property taxes, or other itemizable items you didn’t list. Let me know if you have mortgage interest or sizable property taxes.

7) Other deductions and tax planning opportunities
- Half of self‑employment tax is deductible (Schedule 1).
- Retirement plan contributions (SEP‑IRA, Solo 401(k), or traditional IRA) can reduce taxable income and are especially useful for self‑employed people. If you want to reduce taxable income for 2024, you may still be able to contribute to an IRA by the filing deadline; SEP/Solo 401(k) contributions depend on plan rules and timing.
- Self‑employed health insurance premiums (if you pay for your own) may be deductible as an adjustment to income.

8) Forms you’ll likely need
Federal:
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (for adjustments like student loan interest and one-half SE tax)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $15,000 IT contracting income and related business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if you use the actual method for home office; not needed for the simplified method
- Schedule A (Itemized Deductions) — only if you choose to itemize
- Form 1098‑E (student loan interest) — you should receive this from the servicer showing interest paid in 2024 (used to support the student loan deduction)
- Form 4562 (Depreciation) — only if you claim depreciation (rare for renters, more for homeowners)

State (California):
- CA Form 540 (California Resident Income Tax Return)
- CA Schedule CA (540) — to reconcile federal to California adjustments
- CA has its own rules about certain deductions and conformity; donations and mortgage interest generally carry over, but there are differences — your tax preparer or software will handle those.

9) Recordkeeping
- Keep receipts for charitable donations, home‑office calculations, business expenses, and the 1098‑E (student loan interest).
- Keep copies of all 1099s and W‑2s.

10) Practical recommendations / next steps
- If those $650 unreimbursed expenses are business expenses, treat them as Schedule C deductions; if they’re for your W‑2 job, they’re not deductible.
- If the student loan interest $4,800 was NOT paid in 2024, you cannot deduct it on your 2024 return — only the interest you actually paid in 2024 is deductible.
- Likely outcome: with the information provided and no mortgage interest or large property taxes, you will most likely take the standard deduction (MFJ $29,200) and report the contracting income on Schedule C, use Schedule SE for self‑employment tax, and either take the simplified home office ($650) or the actual allocation if that produces a larger deduction.
- Consider retirement contributions (SEP‑IRA/Solo 401(k)) to lower taxable income if you want to reduce taxes for the year — I can show estimates for contribution limits and tax impact.

