Summary answer first
- Likely better to take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly) rather than itemize, unless you have large state/local taxes or mortgage/other deductible items you haven’t listed.
- Your contracting income should be reported on Schedule C. The home office is a Schedule C business deduction — use the regular method (your rent-allocation) or the simplified method; with the numbers you gave the regular method (rent apportioned to the office) will almost certainly give a larger deduction.
- Student loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (adjustment to income) but the maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (not the full $4,800 you listed) and it phases out at higher incomes.
- Unreimbursed W-2 employee expenses are generally NOT deductible for 2018–2025. However, unreimbursed expenses that are ordinary and necessary for your self-employment (your IT contracting) are deductible on Schedule C.
- Interest from Marcus and Interactive Brokers is taxable and reported on your return; because combined interest/ordinary dividends exceed $1,500, you will likely need Schedule B.

Detailed breakdown and steps

1) Income to report
- W-2 from Desert Sun Logistics: $91,000 — reported on Form 1040 W-2 lines.
- Contracting: $15,000 — report on Schedule C (Form 1040). If you received 1099-NEC/1099-MISC, include those amounts; if no 1099, you still must report the income.
- Interest: $1,780 (Marcus) + $480 (Interactive Brokers) = $2,260 — taxable interest; because total interest > $1,500 you’ll file Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends).

2) Home office treatment
- You said: exclusive home office = 130 sq ft; total home = 780 sq ft. Business-use percentage = 130 / 780 ≈ 16.67%.
- Two methods:
  - Regular (actual expenses): allocate the business percentage of allowable home expenses to Schedule C. For rent only: annual rent = $1,600 × 12 = $19,200 → home-office portion ≈ $3,200 (19,200 × 0.1667). If you can allocate utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., add those shares as well (they increase the deduction).
  - Simplified: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650.
- Recommendation: use the regular method (Form 8829) if you only have rent and it results in a larger deduction ($3,200 > $650). Use Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) or include the calculation on Schedule C. Keep records proving exclusive and regular use and how you calculated square footage and expenses.

3) Student loan interest
- The federal student loan interest deduction is an adjustment on Schedule 1, not an itemized deduction. The maximum is $2,500 per year (subject to income phase-outs). You listed $4,800 — you cannot deduct more than $2,500 for federal. You must check the 1098-E or lender statement to confirm the amount of interest paid in 2024.
- Income phase-out for MFJ applies (check current IRS MAGI thresholds for tax year 2024); with the income you listed (≈$108k) you are likely under the phaseout floor and therefore eligible for the full $2,500, but confirm with exact MAGI.

4) Itemize vs. Standard deduction
- Standard deduction (tax year 2024) for married filing jointly: $29,200 (check the IRS announcement for the tax year you are filing to confirm exact figure).
- Typical itemized components you listed:
  - Charitable cash: $800
  - SALT (state and local income/property taxes): you did not give state tax paid; CA income tax withheld from W-2 could add several thousand, but SALT is capped at $10,000.
  - Mortgage interest/property tax: none listed (you rent).
- Because most of your “above the line” reductions (student loan interest, half SE tax) reduce AGI but do not count toward itemized deductions, and your charitable gift is only $800, itemizing will likely be below the $29k standard deduction unless you have substantial state taxes or other itemizable items you didn’t list. Conclusion: Most likely take the standard deduction.

5) Unreimbursed expenses
- If these $650 are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W-2 job: not deductible (suspended through 2025).
- If they are business expenses for your IT contracting (supplies, software, travel, etc.), deduct them on Schedule C. Please confirm which category they fall into.

6) Self-employment tax and other adjustments
- Your net self-employment income (Schedule C profit) is subject to self-employment (SE) tax (Social Security and Medicare). You compute SE tax on Schedule SE, but you may deduct half of your SE tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- You can also reduce taxable income by contributing to a SEP-IRA or Solo 401(k) if you want to lower tax liability for the year (subject to contribution limits and rules).

7) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- Depending on your net Schedule C profit, you may qualify for the QBI (Section 199A) deduction of up to 20% of qualified business income. There are income thresholds and limitations; with your modest self-employment income, you may well be eligible for a simple QBI calculation (Form 8995 or 8995-A).

8) Forms you will likely need (Federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income/adjustments) — student loan interest deduction and deductible part of self-employment tax appear here
- Schedule B (if interest/dividends > $1,500)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for the $15,000 contracting income and business expenses (including business share of home office, if using regular method)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — if you use the regular (actual expense) home office method
- Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax)
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — QBI deduction (if applicable)
- 1099-NEC / 1099-MISC (income docs) and 1099-INT / 1099-B statements for interest/brokerage items (attach/enter amounts as required)
- State return: California Form 540 (or Form 540NR if nonresident/part-year). Note: California tax law differs in certain adjustments — check CA rules for deductibility of student loan interest and other adjustments.

Documentation to keep
- 1099s and W-2
- Bank/brokerage 1099s and year-end interest/dividend statements
- Lease and rent receipts/statements (for home office allocation)
- Utility/renter’s insurance bills if you plan to allocate more than rent
- Receipts for business expenses (the $650 and any other contractor expenses)
- 1098-E or lender statement showing 2024 student loan interest paid
- Records proving exclusive use of the home office (sketch/floor plan, photos, log)

Quick example calculation (approximate) using the numbers you gave
- Total income before adjustments ≈ $108,260 (91,000 + 15,000 + 1,780 + 480).
- If you deduct home office as rent allocation: home-office deduction ≈ $3,200.
- If the $650 is a business expense, net self-employment income ≈ 15,000 − 3,200 − 650 = $11,150.
- SE tax on that net (approx): about $1,575 (you then deduct half ≈ $788 as an adjustment).
- Student loan interest deduction (max allowable) = up to $2,500 (not $4,800), subject to MAGI limits.
- Charitable $800 only helps if you itemize (probably insufficient to beat the standard deduction unless you have significant state taxes).

Questions I need to give a more precise recommendation / calculation
1. Is the $4,800 the amount of student loan interest you paid in 2024, or is it a historical total? Please confirm the 2024 interest actually paid (and whether you have a 1098-E).
2. Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses related to your self‑employment/IT contracting or are they expenses tied to your W-2 job?
3. How much state income tax (or state/local taxes) did you pay or have withheld in 2024? Do you pay any property taxes?
4. Do you have other itemizable expenses (mortgage interest, large medical expenses, large charitable gifts, or other deductible items) not listed?

Would you like me to
- run a rough numbers worksheet showing estimated taxable income, self-employment tax, and whether itemizing beats the standard deduction (I can do that if you confirm the items above), or
- draft an email to a CPA with these questions and the documents to attach?

