Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — this is enough to give a clear picture and next steps. Summary up-front: you will almost certainly take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly = $29,200). Your contracting income should be reported on Schedule C, where you can take a home-office deduction (regular method looks better than the simplified method here) and deduct business expenses (including the $650 if it’s business-related). Student loan interest is an above-the-line deduction (but limited to $2,500 and subject to income phaseouts). Unreimbursed W-2 employee expenses are not deductible for federal returns (through 2025). You will also owe self-employment tax on net contracting income and should file Schedule SE (half of that SE tax is deductible).

Details and what to do next

1) Which deductions you can claim
- Student loan interest
  - If the $4,800 represents interest you actually paid in 2024, you can claim the student loan interest deduction as an above-the-line adjustment on Form 1040 (reported on Schedule 1), but the maximum annual deduction is $2,500 and it phases out based on MAGI for married filing jointly. I’ll need your MAGI/household income (or confirm whether you expect AGI under ~$150k–$170k) to know whether the full $2,500 is available. You should get Form 1098‑E from your loan servicer if they reported interest paid.

- Charitable gift
  - $800 to Habitat for Humanity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). There is no general above-the-line deduction for cash gifts for 2024.

- Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
  - If these were expenses related to your IT contracting (self-employed) they belong on Schedule C as business expenses.
  - If they were expenses for your W‑2 employment, they are not deductible on the federal return (suspended under TCJA).

- Home office
  - Because you have self-employment (IT contracting), you may use the home office deduction if the space is used regularly and exclusively for business.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft. For 130 sq ft = $650.
    - Regular method: prorate actual home expenses. Your allocation = 130 / 780 = 16.6667%. Annual rent = $1,600 × 12 = $19,200. Rent portion = 19,200 × 16.6667% = $3,200. Add proportionate utilities/insurance/repairs for the space.
  - Conclusion: the regular method will almost certainly give a larger deduction (rent share alone = $3,200 vs $650 simplified), so use the regular method unless you want simplicity.

- Self-employment tax and related
  - Your net income from contracting (Schedule C revenue minus business expenses, including home-office share and the $650 if business-related) is subject to self-employment (SE) tax (Social Security + Medicare). You calculate SE tax on Schedule SE and pay ~15.3% on net earnings (with the 92.35% adjustment applied first).
  - Half of your SE tax is an above-the-line deduction on Form 1040.
  - You should consider estimated quarterly tax payments if not made by your clients.

- Investment and interest income
  - Marcus savings $1,780 + Interactive Brokers $480 = $2,260 of interest. Because total taxable interest > $1,500, you’ll complete Schedule B and report these on Form 1040. Expect 1099‑INT / 1099‑B from those institutions.

2) Standard deduction vs. itemizing
- 2024 standard deduction for married filing jointly: $29,200.
- Likely itemized deductions available to you (based on what you told me): charitable = $800; state income taxes paid (from W-2 withholding or estimated taxes) — even combined, it’s unlikely to reach $29,200 unless you have substantial state tax withholding or other deductible items (mortgage interest, large medical expenses, property tax, etc.). Because you stated you rent (no mortgage interest, no property tax), you will most likely take the standard deduction.
- Net effect: take the standard deduction and still claim the student loan interest (above-the-line) and all Schedule C business deductions plus the SE tax deduction.

3) Forms you will need (Federal)
- Form 1040
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for adjustments such as student loan interest and to report additional income lines that tie to Schedules
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report your $15,000 contracting income and business expenses (home office, business supplies, the $650 if business-related)
- Schedule SE — to calculate self-employment tax
- Schedule B — for interest income (total > $1,500)
- Schedule A — only if you decide to itemize (likely you will not)
- Form 8995 (or 8995-A) — Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction: you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income from your Schedule C (subject to limits and taxable income thresholds). For a small contracting business and modest taxable income, you’ll likely use the simplified Form 8995.
- Keep Forms 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if issued), 1099‑INT, 1098‑E (student loan interest), W‑2

California (state)
- CA Form 540 (resident return) and any CA schedules for adjustments. Most federal amounts flow to California, but there are specific CA adjustments you may need to review with a CA tax guide or preparer.

4) Quick example of how the pieces fit (illustrative estimate)
- Income: W‑2 $91,000 + Contracting $15,000 + Interest $2,260 = $108,260 gross.
- Home office regular rent share: $3,200. If the $650 unreimbursed expenses are business-related, add them as Schedule C expenses. Example net self-employment income = 15,000 − 3,200 − 650 = 11,150 (illustrative).
- SE tax on net: roughly $1,575 (Schedule SE), half (~$788) deductible above-the-line.
- Student loan interest: max deductible up to $2,500 (if you qualify by income).
- Standard deduction $29,200 reduces taxable income.
- QBI deduction may reduce taxable income further (~20% of QBI if eligible).

5) What I need from you to finalize recommendations / calculate exact tax effect
- Confirm whether the $4,800 is interest paid during the 2024 tax year (or something else).
- Confirm whether the $650 unreimbursed expenses were for your contracting business or for your W‑2 job.
- Provide the CA state tax withheld (from W‑2) or total state income taxes paid in 2024 (helps determine whether itemizing could beat the standard deduction).
- Tell me whether you received any 1099‑NEC from clients, or other business income/expenses not listed, and whether you made estimated tax payments.
- Any mortgage interest or property tax (you said you pay rent, so likely none) or large medical expenses?

6) Practical next steps
- Gather documents: W‑2, any 1099‑NEC or records for the $15k, 1099‑INTs, 1098‑E for student loan interest (or statements), receipts for charitable donation, rent amount and utility bills, records of business expenses (receipts), and any estimated tax payments.
- Use the regular home-office method (calculate square footage and prorate rent/utilities) — it will likely produce a larger deduction than the simplified method.
- Report contracting on Schedule C and file Schedule SE; claim half SE tax and student loan interest as above-the-line deductions; take the standard deduction unless your total itemizable deductions exceed $29,200.
- Consider whether you should make/adjust estimated payments for 2025.

If you want, I can:
- Run the numbers with your confirmation of the items I asked about (I can compute estimated taxable income, SE tax, QBI deduction, and whether itemizing would beat the standard deduction), or
- Draft a checklist email you can send to your tax preparer with all required documents.

Which would you like me to do next?

