Key conclusions up front
- You will almost certainly take the standard deduction for federal (Married Filing Jointly standard deduction is $29,200 for 2024) because your itemizable deductions (as stated) are much smaller than $29,200.
- Your self‑employment income and home‑office situation are handled on Schedule C (and Schedule SE for self‑employment tax). The home‑office deduction is available only for the self‑employment portion (not for W‑2 work).
- Student loan interest deduction is an “above‑the‑line” adjustment (reduces AGI) but is capped at $2,500 — so you can’t deduct the full $4,800 paid.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for a W‑2 job are not deductible under current federal law. If the $650 is business expense for your IT contracting, you can deduct it on Schedule C.

Detailed breakdown

1) Standard deduction vs. itemizing
- Standard deduction (MFJ, 2024): $29,200.
- Your reported likely itemized pieces: charitable cash $800. (You didn’t list mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, major medical, or large property tax, so itemized total appears far below $29,200.)
- Recommendation: Take the standard deduction for your federal return. (The home‑office deduction does not factor into the itemize vs standard decision in the usual way because it’s taken on Schedule C if you’re self‑employed.)

2) Student loan interest
- Limit: maximum deductible student loan interest = $2,500 per year (adjustment to income on Form 1040 Schedule 1).
- Income phase‑out: there is a MAGI phaseout for married filing jointly, but with your total income (~$108k) you are under the 2023/2024 phaseout thresholds, so you should be eligible for the full $2,500 (not the full $4,800 you paid).
- Documentation: Form 1098‑E (or loan servicer statement) to show interest paid.

3) Home office
- You qualify for a home office deduction only for the self‑employment (contracting) work, provided the office is used regularly and exclusively for that business (you said it’s exclusive).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 130 sq ft × $5 = $650 (easy, no Form 8829 required).
  b) Actual expense method: allocate actual home expenses by business‑use percentage. Your business percentage = 130 / 780 = 16.6667%. Annual rent = $19,200 → rent portion attributable to the office ≈ $3,200. If you have utility bills, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc., 16.6667% of those can also be allocated to the home office. You’d usually get a larger deduction with the actual method here because the rent portion alone (~$3,200) is > $650.
- Form to use:
  - If you use actual expenses, file Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and attach it to Schedule C.
  - If you use the simplified method, report the $650 deduction on Schedule C (no Form 8829).
- Note: Home office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit, which lowers income subject to both income tax and self‑employment tax.

4) Self‑employment tax and related deductions
- You must file Schedule C (profit/loss from business) for the $15,000 contracting income and report any business expenses (including home office, if applicable).
- You must file Schedule SE to compute self‑employment tax (~15.3% of net SE income, applied to 92.35% of net income). You may owe self‑employment tax in addition to income tax.
- You can deduct “half” of your self‑employment tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (this reduces AGI).
- If you have net self‑employment profit, consider estimated tax payments (quarterly) if taxes are not being withheld.

5) Charitable contribution
- $800 cash to Habitat for Humanity is deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Because your likely itemized total is small, you’ll most likely not itemize and thus will not get an additional federal deduction for this donation. Keep your receipt in case you itemize later or for records.

6) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If these are related to your contracting business, include them as business expenses on Schedule C (e.g., supplies, software, travel).
- If these are unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 job (employee), they are not deductible on your federal return for tax years through 2025 (suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions).

7) Interest and brokerage items
- Bank and brokerage interest totaling $2,260 > $1,500 → you should complete Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) on Form 1040.
- If you have any sales of securities showing capital gains/losses from Interactive Brokers, you’ll need Form 8949 and Schedule D. (You said $480 from IB which sounds like interest — if it’s interest, it goes on Schedule B.)

8) Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction
- With $15k net business income, you may be eligible for the QBI (Section 199A) deduction of up to 20% of qualified business income. With your overall income, you’re likely under the thresholds that complicate the calculation, so you probably qualify for a simple QBI calculation. The QBI deduction is claimed on Form 1040 (Form 8995 or 8995‑A).
- Note: QBI rules are complex in some cases; if you want to maximize this, I can help compute it once net profit and other adjustments are known.

9) State taxes (California)
- You’ll file California Form 540 (or the correct CA individual return) for state taxes. CA generally uses federal AGI with some adjustments. Some federal deductions (like the federal QBI deduction) do not reduce California taxable income in the same way — check CA Form 540 instructions or consult a CA tax pro for state-specific conformity issues.
- CA does not allow the federal student loan interest deduction as an addback? (State conformity details change; I recommend reviewing CA instructions or consulting a preparer.)

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — student loan interest adjustment, half of self‑employment tax adjustment
- Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends) — because interest > $1,500
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business) — report $15k contracting income and business expenses
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax) — compute SE tax
