Thanks — I can walk through how to treat each item, whether you’ll likely itemize or take the standard deduction, the best way to handle the home‑office, and which federal (and state) forms you’ll need. I’ll also flag a few questions where I need more detail to be exact.

Quick summary / bottom line
- Most likely you should take the standard deduction for federal returns (MFJ), because your listed itemizable items (charity $800, etc.) are small versus the standard deduction.
- Report the $15,000 IT contracting on Schedule C (self‑employment). That allows you to take a home office deduction (actual or simplified) and deduct any business expenses there.
- Use the actual home‑office method if you can document rent/utilities etc., because the actual allocation (about $3,200) looks larger than the simplified method ($650).
- You can claim up to $2,500 student‑loan interest as an adjustment to income (subject to MAGI phase‑out). With the income numbers you gave you are likely under the phase‑out and eligible for the full $2,500, but confirm MAGI once all items are entered.
- You will owe self‑employment tax on net contract income and will report that on Schedule SE; you can deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income.
- State rules (California) differ from federal for some items (notably certain above‑the‑line deductions and the QBI deduction). Consider a CA review or a tax preparer for state specifics.

Details and calculations (illustrative)
1) Income to report
- W‑2: $91,000 — reported on Form 1040 (W‑2 income).
- Self‑employment: $15,000 — report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). Combine both clients’ income on one Schedule C unless they are separate businesses (then one Schedule C per business).
- Interest / brokerage: Marcus $1,780 + Interactive Brokers $480 = $2,260. Interest and ordinary dividends are reported on Form 1040; because total interest and ordinary dividends exceed $1,500, you’ll likely need Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends). If any IB amount is capital gain or sale proceeds, you’ll also need Form 8949/Schedule D.

2) Home office (you stated 130 sq ft exclusive; home = 780 sq ft)
- Business‑use percentage = 130 / 780 = 16.667% (≈16.67%).
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction (entered on Schedule C).
  b) Actual method: allocate your actual qualifying home expenses (rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, etc.) by 16.667% and deduct that portion on Form 8829 (attached to Schedule C). Example using only rent:
    - Rent: $1,600 × 12 = $19,200. 19,200 × 16.667% ≈ $3,200.
    - If you have utilities that you pay and can document, add their 16.667% share too.
  Because the rent portion alone (~$3,200) is much larger than the simplified $650, the actual method will probably give you a larger deduction unless you have no other allocable expenses or recordkeeping is a problem.
- Note: You must meet the rules: exclusive and regular use for business and principal place of business (or used to meet clients/customers). Home office deduction is only for self‑employment (Schedule C) and not for W‑2 jobs.

3) Other deductions and adjustments
- Student loan interest: maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (adjustment to income on Schedule 1) and phases out at higher MAGI. Based on the figures you gave (91k W‑2 + 15k self‑employment + 2.26k interest = ~108k gross before SE deduction), after deducting half SE tax you’re likely well under the phase‑out — so you should be able to claim the full $2,500. (Double‑check MAGI once everything is entered.)
- Charitable gift $800: deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). Standard deduction for MFJ is substantially larger than $800, so you’ll almost certainly take the standard deduction unless you have other large itemized items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to $10k SALT cap, large medical expenses, etc.).
- Unreimbursed expenses $650:
  - If these are unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job, they are NOT deductible on federal returns for 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension).
  - If they are business expenses related to your IT contracting (self‑employment), report them on Schedule C — they reduce net self‑employment income.
- QBI (Qualified Business Income) deduction: you may be eligible for up to 20% of your qualified business income from the contracting business (Form 8995 or 8995‑A). Because your contracting income is modest, you’ll likely be able to take the deduction — but California does not conform to the QBI deduction (see state notes below).

4) Self‑employment tax
- Net self‑employment income = gross contracting income minus Schedule C business expenses (including the home office deduction if using Schedule C).
- SE tax = 15.3% on ~92.35% of net self‑employment income (calculated on Schedule SE). You may deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.

Illustrative quick example (approximate)
- Self‑employment gross: $15,000
- Business expenses (if you use actual home office and have no other business costs): Rent allocation ≈ $3,200 + unreimbursed business expenses $650 = $3,850
- Net SE income ≈ $11,150
- SE tax ≈ $11,150 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $1,575 (so half SE ≈ $788 deductible)
- That lowers AGI and reduces income tax and SE tax exposure. This example shows the actual home‑office method would reduce your net SE income significantly vs. the simplified $650.

5) State (California) notes
- California tax rules differ from federal in several areas. For example, California historically does not conform to certain above‑the‑line federal deductions and to the federal QBI deduction — meaning you may owe more taxable income on your CA return. Also check whether CA allows the student‑loan interest adjustment (some states do not). I recommend verifying state treatment (or ask if you want, and I can call out likely CA adjustments more precisely).

6) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (U.S. Individual Income Tax Return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income) — for some adjustments and additional income items
- Schedule B — if interest and ordinary dividends > $1,500 (likely, since your interest total is $2,260)
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — for your IT contracting (report income and business expenses)
- Schedule SE — to calculate self‑employment tax
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the actual method for home‑office). If you use simplified method, you do NOT file Form 8829; report the simplified deduction on Schedule C.
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — Qualified Business Income Deduction (if you claim QBI)
- Schedule A — only if you itemize (from the data shown you’ll likely not use Schedule A)
- If you sold investments or have capital gains/losses: Form 8949 and Schedule D (only if applicable)
- Keep 1099s/1098/etc.: 1099‑NEC or 1099‑MISC (for contractor payments), 1099‑INT (Marcus), 1099‑DIV / 1099‑B (Interactive Brokers) as applicable.

7) Records to keep
- 1099s and W‑2
- Bank/brokerage statements and year‑end forms
- Receipts for charitable gifts (you have the Habitat receipt)
- Documentation of rent, utilities, renter’s insurance, for home‑office allocation (copies of lease and monthly statements)
- Receipts for business expenses and mileage logs (if relevant)
- Student loan interest statement (Form 1098‑E) from Cornerstone — you’ll need that.

What I need from you to be more precise
- Do you rent or own your home? (You said you pay $1,600/month — I assumed rent; if you own, depreciation rules and Form 8829 details differ and depreciation recapture is an issue when you sell.)
- Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses for the W‑2 job or for the IT contracting business?
- Is the $480 from Interactive Brokers interest, dividends, or sale proceeds/capital gains? (This determines Schedule B vs. Schedule D/Form 8949.)
- Do you expect any other itemizable expenses (mortgage interest, property taxes, large medical expenses, or large state taxes) that would push you over the standard deduction?

Recommended next steps
1. If you want, I can run through the numbers with your answers to the questions above and produce a more exact estimate of taxable income and expected federal tax, including an estimated SE tax and the effect of QBI.
2. Consider paying estimated quarterly taxes for both federal and California if you haven’t already had sufficient withholding (to avoid underpayment penalties).
3. If you want state‑specific handling (CA), I can outline the likely California adjustments once you confirm rent vs own and provide the student loan 1098 info.

Would you like me to:
- (A) estimate your federal tax using the figures above and an assumed standard deduction and the actual home‑office method, or
- (B) search for any missing forms/1099s you expect but haven’t received, or
- (C) prepare a checklist of documents to give to a CPA?

Tell me which and answer the quick clarifying questions above and I’ll proceed.

