Below is a concise, practical summary and next steps so you can file correctly. I’ll show what’s deductible, how the home-office works, whether to itemize, and the federal/state forms you’ll most likely need. I’ll also include rough example calculations so you can see the impact — you can give me exact numbers from forms (W‑2 box amounts, 1099s, utility totals) and I’ll run final numbers.

What you can deduct (federal)
- Self-employment business expenses (for the $15,000 IT contracting):
  - Ordinary and necessary expenses directly related to the contracting work (you mentioned $650 unreimbursed expenses — if those are for the contracting work they belong on Schedule C and reduce your self-employment income).
  - Home-office deduction (see details below) — claimed on Schedule C (regular method uses Form 8829; simplified method does not).
- Self-employment tax: you’ll pay self-employment tax (Social Security/Medicare) on net self‑employment earnings, and you may deduct half of that SE tax as an adjustment to income on Form 1040.
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 of student-loan interest is deductible as an above-the-line adjustment to income (Form 1040; usually reported from Form 1098‑E). You reported $4,800 paid, but the maximum federal deduction is $2,500 (and it phases out at higher MAGI — your income appears below typical phaseout levels).
- Charitable contribution: your $800 donation to Habitat for Humanity is an itemized deduction (Schedule A) if you itemize. There is no general federal above-the-line deduction for cash gifts for 2024 (unless a special provision applies).
- Interest income from Marcus and Interactive Brokers must be reported (1099‑INT, 1099‑B as applicable) — those are income, not deductions.

What you cannot deduct (federal)
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job (these were suspended for most employees 2018–2025). If the $650 is unreimbursed expenses for the W‑2 job, it’s NOT deductible. If it was spent for your contracting business, it is deductible on Schedule C.
- Rent you pay (except the business portion allocated to home office). Rent is personal living expense otherwise.

Home office: treatment and recommendation
- You are eligible for a home-office deduction only for the self‑employment work, not for your W‑2 employment.
- Two methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 × square feet used (max 300 sq ft). For 130 sq ft → 130 × $5 = $650.
  - Regular method: pro rata share of actual home expenses. Your allocation = 130 / 780 = 16.6667% of allowable home expenses. With rent $1,600/month → annual rent $19,200 → 16.6667% × $19,200 = $3,200 (plus your share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., if you provide those amounts). Because $3,200 (regular) > $650 (simplified), the regular method will likely give you a bigger deduction. Use Form 8829 to compute the regular-method deduction.
- Remember: home-office deduction is limited to the net profit of the business — it cannot (generally) create or increase a business loss (excess may be suspended and carried forward).

Itemize vs. standard deduction (federal)
- 2024 standard deduction (married filing jointly): $29,200. (If you want absolute certainty I can confirm the number against the exact tax-year table.)
- Your likely itemized deductions (from the details you gave) appear small: $800 charitable + any state/local tax you actually paid (state income tax withheld on the W‑2), mortgage interest/property tax (you’re renting so likely none), medical expenses (not mentioned), etc.
- Conclusion: With only $800 of charitable gifts plus the likely small set of other itemizable items, the federal standard deduction is almost certainly larger — so you will typically take the standard deduction.
- Action: gather W‑2 box 17/state tax withheld and any other deductible items (property tax, significant medical, large charity) — if total itemizable deductions exceed $29,200, itemize; otherwise take the standard deduction.

State (California) notes
- You will file California Form 540.
- California’s rules have differences from federal in certain items (e.g., some federal above-the-line adjustments may not be allowed on the CA return). I recommend checking CA Form 540 instructions or I can look up specific CA treatment if you want. Generally, CA tax is computed from federal AGI with some state-specific adjustments.

Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments to income (e.g., student loan interest, deduction for half of SE tax if needed).
- Schedule C — Profit or Loss from Business (report $15,000 contracting income, minus business expenses including home office and the $650 if it is business-related).
- Schedule SE — Self-Employment Tax (calculate SE tax and the deductible half).
- Form 8829 — Expenses for Business Use of Your Home (if you use the regular method for home-office deduction). If you use the simplified method, you do NOT file Form 8829 — enter the simplified deduction directly on Schedule C.
- Schedule A — Itemized Deductions (only if you choose to itemize; otherwise you won’t file amounts here).
- 1098‑E — student loan interest statement from Cornerstone (they should issue one for interest paid).
- 1099‑INT and/or 1099‑DIV, 1099‑B (from Marcus / Interactive Brokers) — to report interest/dividend and brokerage income.
- 1099‑NEC (if one or both clients issued 1099s for contracting income) — report on Schedule C even if you don’t get a 1099.
- California Form 540 — state return.

Estimated/calendaring items
- Because you have self-employment income, you may owe estimated quarterly tax (Form 1040‑ES) for the year if your withholding does not cover your total tax liability. If this is the first year with contracting income, consider making estimated payments to avoid underpayment penalties.

Rough example calculation (illustrative — plug in exact withholding & expenses for final numbers)
Using your numbers and assuming the $650 is a business expense and using the regular home-office allocation from rent (no other home expenses reported):
- W‑2 wages: $91,000
- Interest income: $2,260 (Marcus $1,780 + IB $480)
- Net self-employment (example): $15,000 − $650 (business expenses) − $3,200 (home-office share of rent) = $11,150
- Total income ≈ $104,410
- Self-employment tax on net SE ≈ $1,575 → half deductible ≈ $788
- Student loan interest deduction limited to $2,500 (not the full $4,800)
- AGI after above-the-line adjustments ≈ $101,122 (approx.)
- Standard deduction $29,200 → estimated taxable income ≈ $71,922 (approx.)

(These are illustrative; give me your W‑2 box 1, box 16, amounts of any utilities or renter’s insurance, exact 1099 numbers and I’ll produce exact final calculations and estimate federal + CA tax owed/refund.)

What I recommend you do next
1. Confirm which of the $650 unreimbursed expenses are business-related (if they are for the contracting work put them on Schedule C; if they were for W‑2 employment they are not deductible).
2. Gather forms: W‑2, 1099‑INT (Marcus), 1099s from Interactive Brokers, any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC from clients, Form 1098‑E from Cornerstone, receipts for the $800 charitable gift, and utility bills / renter’s insurance if you want to use the regular home-office method.
3. Decide home-office method: likely the regular method is better given your rent and office size; I can calculate precisely with utility/insurance numbers.
4. If you expect significant tax due (because of self‑employment tax and reduced withholding), consider making (or increasing) estimated payments for next year.

