Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short answer up front
- Most likely you will take the standard deduction for 2024 (married filing jointly) rather than itemizing, because your listed itemizable expenses (cash charity $800) are small and you didn’t list mortgage interest or large state/property taxes. (The 2024 standard deduction for MFJ is about $29,200 — confirm on IRS.gov when you file.)
- Report the $15,000 contracting as self‑employment income on Schedule C (net business income reduces your AGI and can qualify for the home‑office deduction and the QBI deduction). Self‑employment income is also subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE).
- Home office: you can take a home‑office deduction for the 130 sq ft exclusive office if it’s used regularly and exclusively for the contracting work. You can use either the simplified method or the regular method (Form 8829) — more detail below.
- Student loan interest: up to $2,500 may be deductible as an above‑the‑line deduction (Schedule 1), subject to MAGI phase‑outs. If you really paid $4,800 in interest this year you can only claim up to the limit and must confirm income phaseouts.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses for your W‑2 job generally are not deductible for tax years 2018–2025. The $650 can be deductible only if it’s an ordinary/necessary business expense of the self‑employment business (Schedule C).
- You’ll need Form 1040 plus Schedule C, Schedule SE, and either Form 8829 (regular home office) or the simplified home office method on Schedule C. You may also need Schedule 1 (adjustments like student loan interest and half SE tax), Form 8995/8995‑A (QBI) if applicable, and Schedule A only if you decide to itemize.

Details and calculations (estimates)
1) Income summary (what you reported)
- W‑2 wages: $91,000
- Contracting (self‑employment): $15,000
- Savings interest (Marcus): $1,780
- Broker income (Interactive Brokers): $480
Total gross income ≈ $108,260.

2) Student loan interest
- Federal: up to $2,500 of student‑loan interest paid in the tax year is an “above‑the‑line” deduction (claimed on Schedule 1). It phases out for higher MAGI (married filing jointly phaseouts — check current IRS thresholds for 2024). If your MAGI is well under the phaseout threshold you can claim up to $2,500 even if you paid $4,800.
- You should have Form 1098‑E from the loan servicer showing interest paid. If you don’t have that, keep statements/records.

3) Charitable contribution
- $800 cash gift to Habitat for Humanity is deductible on Schedule A (itemized).
- If you take the standard deduction, you do not separately deduct this on federal return.

4) Unreimbursed expenses / business expenses
- Unreimbursed employee business expenses (for your W‑2 job) are NOT deductible for most taxpayers through 2025.
- If the $650 relates to your IT contracting (supplies, software, internet, travel, etc.), include it as Schedule C business expense. Keep receipts and allocation.

5) Home‑office deduction
- You stated: 130 sq ft office exclusively used, home is 780 sq ft, rent $1,600/month = $19,200/year.
- Office percentage = 130 / 780 = 16.666…% ≈ 16.67%.
- Regular method (Form 8829): deductible portion of rent = 19,200 * 16.67% ≈ $3,200. You can also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. to the business portion. If you use the regular method, complete Form 8829 and transfer the allowed deduction to Schedule C.
- Simplified method: multiply office sq ft by the IRS per‑sq‑ft rate (max 300 sq ft). For 2023 the rate was $5 per sq ft (max $1,500). The 2024 per‑sq‑ft rate may be different — check current IRS guidance. Simplified method is easier (no Form 8829) but may yield a smaller or larger deduction depending on numbers.
- Important: home‑office deduction is only available for self‑employment/independent contractor use (Schedule C). You cannot claim it for a W‑2 job unless very rare exceptions apply.
- Net effect: the home‑office deduction reduces your Schedule C net income and therefore your AGI, and reduces self‑employment tax base as well.

6) Self‑employment tax and QBI
- Your net Schedule C profit (contracting income minus allowed business expenses including home office and the $650 if it’s a business expense) is subject to self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). You pay ~15.3% on 92.35% of net earnings.
- You can deduct one‑half of self‑employment tax as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- You may also be eligible for the Qualified Business Income (QBI) deduction (Section 199A) equal to up to 20% of qualified business income — reported on Form 8995 or 8995‑A; eligibility depends on taxable income and type of business. It’s worth checking because it could reduce your taxable income further.

7) Itemize vs standard (recommendation)
- 2024 MFJ standard deduction ≈ $29,200 (confirm with IRS). Your known potential itemized deductions are small: $800 charity; you didn’t list mortgage interest or substantial state/property taxes (SALT cap $10,000). So it’s very likely the standard deduction will be larger — therefore take the standard deduction.
- Note: the home‑office deduction is not an itemized deduction — it’s taken on Schedule C whether or not you itemize.

8) Forms you will need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments) — for student loan interest (adjustment) and half of SE tax, other adjustments
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — report $15,000 and business expenses (including home office deduction if using simplified you just claim it on Schedule C; if regular method use Form 8829)
- Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) — only if using the regular method
- Schedule SE (Self‑Employment Tax)
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A (QBI deduction) — if applicable
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize
- Attach Forms 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC (if you received them), Form W‑2, Form 1099‑INT, 1099-B or consolidated broker 1099 as applicable, Form 1098‑E (student loan interest statement).

State (California)
- File California Form 540 (or 540NR if nonresident/part‑year). California often has differences from federal (e.g., different treatment of certain adjustments). Check California FTB guidance or ask a CA tax preparer if you need state‑specific adjustments.

What I need from you to give a precise calculation or prepare a draft worksheet
- Did you actually pay $4,800 of student loan interest in 2024 and do you have Form 1098‑E?
- Were the $15,000 payments reported to you on 1099‑NEC or were you paid some other way? Did you have any other business expenses beyond the $650?
- Do you want me to estimate your federal tax liability (including SE tax and estimated payments) using the numbers above?
- Would you like me to compare the regular vs simplified home‑office methods with exact numbers (I’ll need your annual utilities, renter’s insurance, repair expenses attributable to the home)?

Practical next steps
1. Gather documents: W‑2, any 1099s, 1099‑INT, broker 1099, Form 1098‑E, receipts for charitable donations, receipts for business expenses, rent statements and utility bills, and documentation showing the 130 sq ft office area.
2. Decide whether you want me to run numbers (I can estimate AGI, SE tax, tax liability, and show the effect of using the regular vs simplified home‑office method).
3. Consider consulting a tax preparer or CPA for final filing — especially given self‑employment income, home‑office choices, and potential QBI and CA state differences.

If you want, tell me:
- whether the $650 is a business expense for the contracting work,
- whether you have Form 1098‑E showing student loan interest paid in 2024,
- and whether you want a numeric estimate comparing simplified vs regular home‑office methods and a projected tax bill/refund — I’ll run the numbers.

