Quick summary up front
- Likely best to take the standard deduction for 2024 for married filing jointly (unless you have significant additional itemizable amounts you didn’t list). Based on what you gave, your listed itemizable items are small.
- Your 15k IT contracting is treated as self‑employment income — report it on Schedule C and pay self‑employment tax (Schedule SE). The home‑office deduction applies only to that self‑employment income (not your W‑2 job).
- Student loan interest: maximum federal “above‑the‑line” deduction is limited (generally $2,500 max and phased out at higher MAGI). With your income level you’re likely eligible for the full allowable amount, but the full $4,800 you paid is not deductible.
- You will need Form 1040 plus Schedule 1, Schedule C, Schedule SE, and either Form 8829 (actual-expense method for home office) or use the simplified home‑office method.

Details and how to treat each item

1) Income
- W‑2 wages from Desert Sun Logistics ($91,000): report on Form 1040 (W‑2 will supply the exact amount of federal and state withholding).
- Contracting income ($15,000): report on Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business). You can deduct ordinary and necessary business expenses on Schedule C.
- Interest income ($1,780 Marcus + $480 Interactive Brokers = $2,260): report as taxable interest on Form 1040 (you should receive 1099‑INT/1099‑B or 1099‑INT/1099‑DIV).

2) Self‑employment tax and deductions for the contracting work
- You will owe self‑employment (SE) tax (~15.3% on net self‑employment earnings) reported on Schedule SE. You may deduct half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1.
- Business expenses: your $650 unreimbursed expenses — if they were incurred for the contracting work, include them on Schedule C. If they were unreimbursed expenses for your W‑2 employer job, they are NOT deductible (TCJA suspended miscellaneous itemized deductions).

3) Home office
- Qualification: you said the 130 sq ft is used exclusively for business — that meets the basic test for a home office for self‑employment.
- Methods:
  - Simplified method: $5.00 per sq ft up to 300 sq ft → 130 * $5 = $650 deduction (no Form 8829).
  - Actual‑expense method: allocate a percentage of home expenses by square footage. Your ratio = 130 / 780 = 16.667%. Annual rent = $1,600 * 12 = $19,200. Rental portion allocable to business = $19,200 * 16.667% = $3,200. You may also allocate a portion of utilities, renter’s insurance, internet (business portion), and repairs. Use Form 8829 to compute and report the deduction if you choose actual expenses.
- Which to choose: compare the two; the actual method appears to give more (rent portion alone ~$3,200 > $650). Use actual expenses if you can document the amounts.
- Important: home office deduction reduces your net Schedule C income. It cannot create a deductible loss from the business beyond passive activity or hobby rules — but with $15k gross it’s unlikely that will be an issue.

4) Student loan interest
- The federal deduction for student loan interest is an adjustment to income (reported on Schedule 1). The maximum allowed is generally $2,500 per year (subject to AGI phaseouts). You reported $4,800 paid — you can only deduct up to the allowable limit (and only to the extent the loan and payments qualify). Based on your combined income, you are likely below the phaseout threshold and so may get the full allowable deduction (not the full $4,800).
- Keep Form 1098‑E from Cornerstone or your loan statements as proof.

5) Charitable contribution ($800)
- Cash gift to a qualifying organization is deductible only if you itemize. If you take the standard deduction, you cannot also deduct this. (There is no general federal above‑the‑line charitable deduction for 2024.)

6) Itemize vs standard deduction
- 2024 standard deduction (married filing jointly) is substantially larger than in prior years (confirm the exact IRS published amount for 2024 when you file). Based on the items you listed:
  - Charitable $800
  - No mortgage interest or property tax listed
  - SALT (state income tax paid) and other deductible items not provided
  → it is likely the standard deduction will exceed your itemized total. So you will probably take the standard deduction.
- Action: add up all possible itemized items you paid in 2024 (state income taxes withheld or estimated tax payments, property tax if any, mortgage interest, large charitable gifts, medical expenses over threshold, etc.). If their sum exceeds the standard deduction, itemize; otherwise take the standard deduction.

7) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 (main return)
- Schedule 1 (additional income and adjustments — student loan interest adjustment, half of SE tax deduction)
- Schedule C (report contracting income and business expenses)
- Schedule SE (calculate and report self‑employment tax)
- Form 8829 (if you use the actual expense method for business use of home). If you use simplified method, you do not need 8829.
- Keep 1099‑NEC (if you received any for contracting), W‑2, 1099‑INT(s), 1098‑E (student loan interest statement), and receipts for charitable donations and business expenses.

8) California (state) tax
- You will file California Form 540 (CA resident return). California generally starts from federal AGI with some differences. California does not conform to all federal rules; check state specifics (home office deduction conforms to federal for self‑employment, but some adjustments may apply). Keep state forms and withholding info.

9) Estimated tax / withholding
- Since your contracting income likely had little/no withholding, you may need to pay estimated taxes during the year (or make a larger withholding on your W‑2) to avoid underpayment penalties. For 2024 filing of 2024 taxes, make estimated payments during 2024 if you expect similar income next year. For your 2024 return now, if you underpaid, you might owe tax and possibly penalties.

10) Records to keep
- W‑2, 1099‑NEC or records of contract payments, 1099‑INTs
- 1098‑E or loan statements for student loan interest
- Receipts for charitable contributions (written acknowledgment for cash gifts)
- Rent receipts, utility bills, floor plan or documentation to support square‑foot calculation for home office
- Receipts/invoices for business expenses, mileage logs if applicable
- Any 1099s showing state tax withholding

A few clarifying questions
- Are the $650 unreimbursed expenses related to the contracting work or to your W‑2 employment?
- Do you have mortgage interest, property tax, or significant state taxes paid in 2024 that you didn’t mention?
- Did you receive any 1099‑NEC for the $15,000 or did the clients report it otherwise?

If you want, I can
- Walk you through a rough tax‑estimate using the numbers you provided (estimate federal tax and self‑employment tax), or
- Draft a checklist of documents to collect for filing, or
- Help prepare a structured summary you can give to a tax preparer.

