- Federal: You will almost certainly take the standard deduction (MFJ standard deduction for 2024 is $29,200) — your itemizable deductions as stated (charity $800 + small or no other itemizable items shown) are well under that.
- You can (and should) report your $15,000 contracting income on Schedule C and claim a home‑office deduction for the 130 sq ft space used exclusively for that business. Use Form 8829 (actual expenses) or the simplified method ($5/sq ft) on Schedule C. The home‑office deduction will lower both income tax and self‑employment tax.
- You can deduct up to $2,500 of student‑loan interest (it's an above‑the‑line adjustment on Form 1040/Schedule 1) if your modified AGI is below the phaseout threshold — with your numbers you are almost certainly under the phaseout, so you should get the full $2,500.
- Report interest/ dividends from Marcus and Interactive Brokers as interest/dividend income (Form 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV / Schedule 1).
- Likely required federal forms: Form 1040, Schedule 1, Schedule C, Schedule SE, Form 8829 (if using actual home office method) or simplified calculation entered on Schedule C, and possibly Form 8995/8995‑A (QBI) if you want to claim the Qualified Business Income deduction.
- State: file California Form 540; compare CA standard vs itemized (CA standard deduction is much smaller than the federal; CA has its own rules).  

Detailed breakdown and actions

1) Income to report
- W‑2 wages: $91,000 → reported on Form 1040 (W‑2).
- Contracting (self‑employment) income: $15,000 → report on Schedule C.
  - If you received any 1099‑NEC/1099‑MISC for that work include them; you must report the income even if you did not receive a 1099.
- Interest from Marcus: $1,780 and Interactive Brokers: $480 → report as interest/dividend income on Schedule 1 (attach 1099‑INT / 1099‑DIV statements).
Total gross income shown: $108,260.

2) Home office deduction (self‑employment only)
- Qualification: you said the space (130 sq ft) is used exclusively and regularly for business — that meets the basic test for a home‑office deduction for self‑employed work.
- Two methods:
  a) Simplified method: $5 × 130 sq ft = $650 deduction (easy recordkeeping).
  b) Actual method: business portion = 130 / 780 = 16.6667% of allowable home expenses. Example for rent: $1,600/mo × 12 = $19,200 × 16.6667% = $3,200 of rent would be deductible as a business expense. Add appropriate share of utilities, renter’s insurance, repairs, etc. If you use the actual method you must complete Form 8829 and keep records (bills, lease, utility records) to substantiate the allocation.
- Which to use: based on rent alone, actual method ($3,200) beats simplified ($650). If you have no other allocable home expenses, actual rent share will usually be larger. Use actual if you have documentation and want the larger deduction.
- Effect: the home‑office deduction reduces Schedule C net profit and thus reduces both income tax and self‑employment tax (computed on Schedule SE). Note: home‑office deduction cannot exceed net business income for the activity; excess can carry forward (Form 8829 rules).

3) Student loan interest
- You reported $4,800 paid. The federal student loan interest deduction is capped at $2,500 per return (above‑the‑line adjustment on Schedule 1) and phases out for higher MAGI. With the income figures you gave (approx MAGI in the ~$108k range before adjustments), you should be eligible for the full $2,500 deduction. You will need Form 1098‑E (or a lender statement) to substantiate interest paid.

4) Charitable donation
- $800 to Habitat for Humanity with a receipt is an ordinary charitable deduction. It only helps if you itemize on Schedule A; since the federal standard deduction for MFJ (2024) is $29,200 and your itemized total appears to be much less, you will likely NOT itemize for federal — so the $800 likely won’t reduce your federal taxable income unless you have other itemizable items you didn’t list (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes, medical expenses above threshold, etc.).

5) Unreimbursed expenses ($650)
- If these were unreimbursed employee expenses (you as an employee for Desert Sun Logistics) they are NOT deductible on the federal return for tax years 2018–2025 (TCJA suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions).
- If instead these $650 are ordinary and necessary business expenses related to your contracting work (e.g., supplies, software, travel, phone for the clients), report them on Schedule C and they are deductible against the contracting income. Clarify which they are and keep receipts.

6) Self‑employment tax, QBI, and estimated taxes
- Self‑employment tax: you’ll owe SE tax (Social Security + Medicare) on net Schedule C earnings. Rough estimate: if net self‑employment income (after allowable expenses including home‑office) is roughly $11k–$13k, SE tax will be in the low thousands. Half of the SE tax is deductible as an adjustment on Schedule 1.
- QBI (qualified business income) deduction: you may be eligible for up to 20% of qualified business income from your Schedule C business under IRC 199A. Because your taxable income (after standard deduction) is likely below the threshold, you likely can claim some or most of the QBI deduction. This reduces taxable income (not self‑employment tax) and is calculated on Form 8995 or 8995‑A.
- Quarterly estimated payments: If you expect to owe $1,000+ in tax when you file, you should be making quarterly estimated tax payments so you avoid underpayment penalties. Your employer W‑2 withholding reduces estimated tax obligations; check after preparing a draft return.

7) Itemize vs standard deduction (federal)
- Federal standard deduction (MFJ, 2024): $29,200.
- Your potential itemized items shown: charitable $800. Student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment (does not go on Schedule A). Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible. You likely do not exceed $29,200 itemized. Recommendation: take the standard deduction for federal unless you have additional large itemizable items (mortgage interest, large state/local taxes up to SALT $10k, medical expenses over 7.5% of AGI, large charitable giving beyond $29k).
- For California: CA’s standard deduction is much smaller; CA has different adjustments and limitations. You should compute CA Form 540 both ways (CA standard vs CA itemized) — CA does not conform to all federal rules, so consult CA adjustments or a preparer.

8) Forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040
