Summary of the numbers you gave (tax year 2024)
- W-2 wages (Desert Sun Logistics): $91,000
- Self‑employment (IT contracting, 2 clients): $15,000
- Interest/other investment income: $1,780 (Marcus) + $480 (Interactive Brokers) = $2,260
- Home: rented, 780 sq ft; home office 130 sq ft exclusive
- Rent: $1,600/mo → $19,200/year
- Deductions you listed: $4,800 student loan interest (paid in 2024?), $800 to Habitat for Humanity (cash charitable), $650 unreimbursed expenses (unclear whether business)
- Filing status: Married filing jointly (San Diego, CA)

What you can and cannot deduct (federal)
- Self‑employment business deductions (Schedule C)
  - Report your $15,000 contracting revenue on Schedule C.
  - Ordinary and necessary business expenses for the contracting work are deductible on Schedule C (e.g., supplies, business portion of phone/internet, the business portion of your rent if you use the actual home‑office allocation, the $650 if those are business expenses). If the $650 are unreimbursed employee expenses (for a W‑2 job), they are NOT deductible for tax years 2018–2025 because of TCJA suspension.
  - If you receive $600+ from a client, they should issue a Form 1099‑NEC; report the income even if you don’t receive one.

- Home office
  - Available only for self‑employed use (Schedule C) or special categories of employees (rare). You said the 130 sq ft is exclusive, so you likely meet the exclusive/regular use test for your contracting work.
  - Two methods:
    - Simplified: $5 × square feet, up to 300 sq ft. Here: 130 × $5 = $650.
    - Actual: prorate actual home expenses by office %. Office % = 130/780 = 1/6 ≈ 16.667%. Annual rent $19,200 × 1/6 = $3,200. Plus you could include a prorated share of utilities, renter’s insurance, etc., on the actual method. Because your actual allocation of rent ($3,200) is larger than the simplified $650, the actual method will likely be better.
  - If you use the actual method, you’ll generally complete Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home) and carry the result to Schedule C. (Simplified method is entered directly on Schedule C.)

- Self‑employment tax
  - You’ll owe self‑employment tax (Social Security + Medicare) on your net Schedule C income (calculated on Schedule SE). You can deduct one half of the SE tax as an adjustment to income on Schedule 1 (Form 1040).

- Student loan interest
  - The student loan interest deduction is an above‑the‑line adjustment (Schedule 1) with a maximum of $2,500 per return (not $4,800). So even if you paid $4,800, at most $2,500 may be claimed on your federal return and it phases out at higher MAGI. Whether you can claim the full $2,500 depends on your modified adjusted gross income (MAGI); with the incomes shown (rough estimate below) you’ll likely be under the phaseout, but confirm MAGI carefully.

- Charitable contributions
  - Cash gifts to qualified charities are deductible only if you itemize (Schedule A). If you take the standard deduction, you do not get a separate federal deduction for that $800.

- Interest/Investment income reporting
  - Interest > $1,500 (combined) requires Schedule B. Your total interest (and ordinary dividends, if any) is > $1,500, so you’ll prepare Schedule B and include interest on Form 1040.
  - Interactive Brokers amounts could be interest, dividends, or capital gains — report them as shown on your 1099s; capital gains could require Schedule D.

Will you itemize or take the standard deduction?
- For tax year 2024, the standard deduction for married filing jointly is large (about $29,200).
- Typical itemized deductions include: mortgage interest, state & local taxes (SALT, capped at $10,000), property tax, charitable gifts, large medical expenses (above 7.5% of AGI), etc.
- From the items you listed, your only clear Schedule A item would be $800 charitable. The home office and student loan interest are NOT Schedule A items (student loan interest is an above‑the‑line adjustment). Unless you have substantial mortgage interest, high SALT, big medical expenses, or other large itemizable items, the standard deduction will almost certainly be larger than your itemized total.
- Recommendation: Take the federal standard deduction unless you have other sizable itemizable items not listed (mortgage interest, substantial CA state taxes/property taxes, very large charitable gifts, etc.). Note: home office deduction and QBI are separate from the decision to itemize — they benefit you regardless (they reduce your business/net income and taxable income).

Qualified Business Income (QBI / 20% pass‑through deduction)
- Your net self‑employment income (after business deductions) may qualify for the 20% QBI deduction (Form 8995/8995‑A), subject to limits. Given the income levels you provided, you’re likely under the QBI phaseout and may be eligible for the full 20% of qualified business income (QBI). This is complex enough that I recommend calculating it when you have net profit numbers; tax software or a CPA can confirm.

What forms you will likely need (federal)
- Form 1040 — main return.
- Schedule 1 (Form 1040) — for adjustments (e.g., student loan interest deduction, half SE tax if applicable).
- Schedule B — if interest/dividend ordinary income > $1,500 (your interest totals > $1,500).
- Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business) — to report contracting income and business expenses, and where the home office deduction (actual or simplified) is taken.
- Schedule SE — to compute self‑employment tax.
- Form 8829 — if you use the actual method for the home office (calculates allowable portion of rent, utilities, etc.). If you use the simplified method, you do not file 8829.
- Form 8995 or 8995‑A — for the QBI deduction if you claim it.
- Schedule A — only if you choose to itemize (likely not, given the standard deduction).
- Form 1040-ES — for estimated tax payments during the year if you owe tax after withholding (see recommendation below).

California state return
- Form 540 (California resident return) — file CA taxes. CA largely conforms to federal treatment of Schedule C and business deductions, but there are differences in some adjustments and credits. CA may treat some federal adjustments differently — double‑check CA instructions (or ask a tax pro) about student loan interest and other specific addbacks/subtractions.

Estimated taxes / withholding
- Because you have self‑employment income, you likely owe self‑employment tax and may owe additional income tax. If the additional tax owed (after withholding) is expected to be $1,000+ for the year, you should make estimated quarterly payments (Form 1040‑ES) or increase withholding on W‑2 wages to avoid penalties.
- If your W‑2 withholding covers most of your tax, you may not need large estimated payments — compute a projection.

Important limits and caveats
- Student loan interest max deduction is $2,500 and phases out by MAGI — check your MAGI. You cannot deduct more than the allowed limit.
- Home office (actual method) cannot create a net loss that offsets other income except to the extent rules allow — excess home office loss may be limited and carried forward. If your Schedule C shows a loss, special rules apply; I can help run the numbers.
- Unreimbursed employee expenses are not deductible (suspended through 2025). If the $650 is business expense for your contracting work, deduct it on Schedule C; if it’s unreimbursed under your W‑2 job, it’s not deductible.
- Keep good records (copies of rent receipts, allocation calculation, receipts for charitable donations, Form 1099s, bank/broker 1099s, student loan interest statement Form 1098‑E if available).

Quick estimated outcome (rough)
